---
/* Site-wide persistent backdrop: a stylized cosmic vista, six objects
   mapped 1:1 to the site's six sections (About=star, Projects=planetary
   system, Playground=nebula, Music=pulsar, Photography=distant galaxy,
   Contact=probe/signal). Mounted once in BaseLayout as the first child of
   the body element with transition:persist, so the WebGL context and
   camera state survive client-side navigation between pages rather than
   being rebuilt from scratch on every route (this component's own inline
   script genuinely only ever runs once per tab session because of that
   persistence; there is no re-init guard needed the way there is for
   non-persisted scripts elsewhere in the layout). Fixed + negative z-index: painting order puts
   negative-z positioned descendants before a stacking context's own
   non-positioned in-flow content, so this always sits behind Header/
   main/Footer regardless of DOM order, without those needing z-index too.

   Glow reuses HomeEmblem's proven technique: additive sprites carrying
   their own alpha (a radial gradient baked into a canvas texture), not
   EffectComposer bloom, since bloom's blurred halo doesn't reliably extend
   into pixels that started fully transparent on an alpha:true canvas. */
---

<div class="home-cosmos" id="home-cosmos" aria-hidden="true" data-cosmos-mode="hero" transition:persist transition:name="cosmos-scene">
  <canvas id="home-cosmos-canvas"></canvas>
  <div class="cosmos-scrim"></div>
  <div class="cosmos-glare-mask"><div class="cosmos-glare" id="cosmos-glare"></div></div>
</div>

<style>
  .home-cosmos {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: -1;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .home-cosmos canvas {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    display: block;
  }

  /* Backdrop scrim: on inner pages the cosmos still has to sit behind real
     body text, but a flat wash across the whole viewport was what turned
     the scene into wallpaper - it dimmed the section's own object just as
     hard as the empty sky around it. This is a horizontal ramp instead:
     dense across the left ~three-quarters, where the centered 70ch content
     column actually lives, and thinning out toward the right margin, which
     is exactly where the script below frames the section's object on inner
     pages. Text keeps its contrast; the object you travelled to stays
     genuinely present in the room beside it rather than being flattened
     into a backdrop.

     Toggled by data-cosmos-mode, set in the script below from the current
     route on every astro:page-load (never from a template conditional on
     this element - see the transition:persist note in the frontmatter
     comment above: this node is never re-rendered by Astro after the first
     paint, only ever updated by client JS).

     Much lighter than it was (62/60/34/6%), because it is no longer the only
     thing carrying legibility - see .cosmos-glare below. A fixed wash strong
     enough for the worst case is by definition too strong for every other
     case, and this one was set for the worst: text crossing the sun's limb,
     which measured 1.4:1 against a 4.5:1 requirement. It failed there anyway
     and flattened the scene everywhere else to do it. */
  .cosmos-scrim {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: linear-gradient(
      to right,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-bg) 40%, transparent) 0%,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-bg) 38%, transparent) 70%,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-bg) 20%, transparent) 86%,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-bg) 4%, transparent) 100%
    );
    opacity: 0;
    transition: opacity 500ms ease;
  }

  /* ---------- Adaptive glare shield ----------
     The scrim above is a fixed gradient: it does not know where the object
     is, so it darkens the empty left margin exactly as hard as the patch of
     sun a paragraph is written across. This is the half that knows.

     A soft radial darkening is parked on the focused object's own projected
     position and sized to its projected radius, then masked to the reading
     column - so it only ever paints where text and object actually overlap,
     and the object's free side stays at full brightness. Sun behind the
     prose: the text sits in a pool of shade that follows it. Probe out in
     the right margin: the mask clips the whole thing away and nothing is
     dimmed at all.

     Position and size come from HomeCosmos's own camera each frame, via
     transform and opacity only - both compositor properties, so this costs
     no layout and no repaint however fast the camera is moving. */
  .cosmos-glare-mask {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    opacity: 0;
    transition: opacity 500ms ease;
    /* Where the content column ends, measured from the real <section> on
       resize and navigation (see measureColumn below). Past the fade stop
       the shield does not exist, which is what keeps this from becoming
       another full-width wash. */
    -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(
      to right,
      #000 0%,
      #000 var(--cosmos-column-edge, 74%),
      transparent var(--cosmos-column-fade, 94%)
    );
    mask-image: linear-gradient(
      to right,
      #000 0%,
      #000 var(--cosmos-column-edge, 74%),
      transparent var(--cosmos-column-fade, 94%)
    );
  }

  .home-cosmos[data-cosmos-mode="backdrop"] .cosmos-glare-mask {
    opacity: 1;
  }

  /* A unit disc, scaled and translated into place. 1000px across so the
     gradient is rasterized at a useful resolution before being scaled -
     a small element blown up several times over bands visibly. */
  .cosmos-glare {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    width: 1000px;
    height: 1000px;
    margin: -500px 0 0 -500px;
    opacity: 0;
    background: radial-gradient(
      closest-side,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-bg) 94%, transparent) 0%,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-bg) 82%, transparent) 40%,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-bg) 40%, transparent) 72%,
      transparent 100%
    );
    will-change: transform, opacity;
  }

  /* Narrow viewports have no right margin to spare - the content column
     runs edge to edge - so there the ramp would only be putting starfield
     behind the last words of every line. Fall back to the flat wash. */
  @media (max-width: 1099px) {
    .cosmos-scrim {
      /* Lightened alongside the ramp above, and for the same reason: this
         was 70% back when a fixed wash was the only mechanism, and leaving
         it there meant the glare shield stacked on top of an unchanged
         worst-case scrim. Between them the star went out - a flat grey-brown
         disc where a photosphere should be. */
      background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-bg) 56%, transparent);
    }
  }

  .home-cosmos[data-cosmos-mode="backdrop"] .cosmos-scrim {
    opacity: 1;
  }

  /* Whether the six-object scene exists is decided once per tab (see
     isDesktop below), but the homepage layout is decided by a live media
     query - so narrowing a desktop window past the breakpoint leaves a
     full-brightness nebula sitting directly behind the plain text menu
     that replaces the journey. Nothing else puts unscrimmed body copy over
     these objects. Keyed on data-cosmos-objects so a visitor who *loaded*
     narrow, and therefore has only the faint starfield back there, doesn't
     get a scrim they never needed. */
  @media (max-width: 899px) {
    .home-cosmos[data-cosmos-objects] .cosmos-scrim {
      opacity: 1;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  import * as THREE from "three";
  import { GLTFLoader } from "three/examples/jsm/loaders/GLTFLoader.js";
  import { navigate } from "astro:transitions/client";
  import { detectDeviceTier, getStarfieldProfile } from "../lib/cosmos/deviceTier";
  import { createStarfield } from "../lib/cosmos/starfield";
  import { createCameraRig } from "../lib/cosmos/cameraRig";
  import { createTransitDust, type TransitDust } from "../lib/cosmos/transitDust";
  import { createSkyEvents } from "../lib/cosmos/skyEvents";
  import { applyGalaxyShear, applyNebulaDrift, createMotionClock } from "../lib/cosmos/pointMotion";
  import { MAX_STEP, easeFactor } from "../lib/cosmos/timing";
  import { DESTINATIONS } from "../lib/destinations";

  const LOG = "[HomeCosmos]";

  try {
    const container = document.getElementById("home-cosmos");
    const canvasEl = document.getElementById("home-cosmos-canvas") as HTMLCanvasElement | null;
    if (!container || !canvasEl) {
      throw new Error("Cosmos container or canvas not found.");
    }

    const reduceMotion = window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches;
    const tier = detectDeviceTier();
    const starfieldProfile = getStarfieldProfile(tier);
    // The six narrative objects (star/planets/nebula/pulsar/galaxy/probe)
    // stay part of the desktop menu experience for now, same as before
    // this phase - not re-litigating that layout on mobile yet. The
    // starfield foundation below is new and runs everywhere, tiered down.
    // Read once at init rather than tracked live: this component only
    // ever initializes once per tab (transition:persist), so a resize
    // across the breakpoint mid-session won't retroactively add/remove
    // the six-object scene - a known, acceptable limit for this phase.
    const isDesktop = window.innerWidth >= 900;

    const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ canvas: canvasEl, antialias: true, alpha: true });
    renderer.setClearColor(0x000000, 0);
    renderer.setPixelRatio(Math.min(window.devicePixelRatio || 1, starfieldProfile.pixelRatioCap));
    renderer.toneMapping = THREE.ACESFilmicToneMapping;
    renderer.toneMappingExposure = 1.1;
    renderer.outputColorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace;

    const scene = new THREE.Scene();
    // Far plane reaches past the galaxy waypoint (~4000 units out, 600
    // across); near plane is pulled back from 0.1 to 0.5 to claw back depth
    // precision over that range - safe because the closest the camera ever
    // parks is ~0.9 units from the probe's near edge.
    const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(50, 1, 0.5, 20000);
    camera.position.set(0, 0, 13);
    camera.lookAt(0, 0, 0);

    function readVarColor(name: string, fallback: string): THREE.Color {
      const raw = getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue(name).trim();
      return new THREE.Color(raw || fallback);
    }

    // Derive palette siblings from the theme accent via hue rotation, rather
    // than hardcoding extra colors, so the nebula/galaxy stay in sync with
    // theme changes exactly like every other material here.
    function hueShift(color: THREE.Color, deltaTurns: number, satMul = 1, lightAdd = 0): THREE.Color {
      const hsl = { h: 0, s: 0, l: 0 };
      color.getHSL(hsl);
      const out = new THREE.Color();
      out.setHSL(
        (hsl.h + deltaTurns + 1) % 1,
        THREE.MathUtils.clamp(hsl.s * satMul, 0, 1),
        THREE.MathUtils.clamp(hsl.l + lightAdd, 0, 1),
      );
      return out;
    }

    function makeGlowTexture(): THREE.CanvasTexture {
      const size = 128;
      const c = document.createElement("canvas");
      c.width = size;
      c.height = size;
      const ctx = c.getContext("2d")!;
      const gradient = ctx.createRadialGradient(size / 2, size / 2, 0, size / 2, size / 2, size / 2);
      gradient.addColorStop(0, "rgba(255,255,255,1)");
      gradient.addColorStop(0.35, "rgba(255,255,255,0.45)");
      gradient.addColorStop(1, "rgba(255,255,255,0)");
      ctx.fillStyle = gradient;
      ctx.fillRect(0, 0, size, size);
      const tex = new THREE.CanvasTexture(c);
      tex.needsUpdate = true;
      return tex;
    }
    const glowTexture = makeGlowTexture();

    // Additive blending that leaves the destination *alpha* alone.
    //
    // THREE.AdditiveBlending adds both colour and alpha. The renderer here
    // is alpha:true (the page's own background gradients show through the
    // canvas), so anything which raises alpha while contributing almost no
    // colour makes those pixels composite *darker* than the page behind
    // them. Bright sprites never revealed this; a large surface deliberately
    // fading to black did - the pulsar's beams grew a dark ellipse at the
    // exact end that was supposed to have faded out of existence.
    //
    // Spread as a spec object rather than a helper so it reads inline at
    // each material that needs it.
    const ADDITIVE_RGB_ONLY = {
      blending: THREE.CustomBlending,
      blendEquation: THREE.AddEquation,
      blendSrc: THREE.SrcAlphaFactor,
      blendDst: THREE.OneFactor,
      blendEquationAlpha: THREE.AddEquation,
      blendSrcAlpha: THREE.ZeroFactor,
      blendDstAlpha: THREE.OneFactor,
    } as const;

    // The same additive pass, but letting alpha accumulate as well.
    //
    // ADDITIVE_RGB_ONLY exists because a large soft surface fading to black
    // raises destination alpha while contributing almost no colour, which
    // composites *darker* than the page behind it. Correct for coronas and
    // haze. Wrong for anything small and bright drawn over empty space: on a
    // premultiplied, transparent canvas it writes bright RGB onto a pixel
    // whose alpha stays zero, and RGB greater than alpha is not a legal
    // premultiplied colour. What happens next is up to the compositor.
    // Chromium's offscreen capture path keeps the colour, which is why this
    // survives every headless screenshot; a real display compositor clamps
    // the colour to the alpha, and alpha is zero, so the pixel is black.
    //
    // The visible signature is unmistakable once you know it: the object
    // only appears where something else has already raised alpha - hugging
    // a glow, or crossing a planet - and is simply absent everywhere else.
    // Brightening it cannot help, because the colour was never the problem.
    const ADDITIVE_WITH_ALPHA = {
      blending: THREE.CustomBlending,
      blendEquation: THREE.AddEquation,
      blendSrc: THREE.SrcAlphaFactor,
      blendDst: THREE.OneFactor,
      blendEquationAlpha: THREE.AddEquation,
      blendSrcAlpha: THREE.OneFactor,
      blendDstAlpha: THREE.OneFactor,
    } as const;

    // Real astronomical materials: NASA/Solar-System-Scope sourced textures
    // and a real scanned asteroid mesh (see public/textures/cosmos/License.txt
    // and public/models/cosmos/License.txt for sourcing + license, and the
    // credit line in Footer.astro). Loaded async and assigned directly as
    // each material's map/scene - three.js updates the texture in place once
    // decoded, so a slow or failed load just leaves today's flat/procedural
    // look in place rather than blocking or breaking the scene.
    const cosmosAssetBase = `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}`.replace(/\/?$/, "/");
    const textureLoader = new THREE.TextureLoader();
    const gltfLoader = new GLTFLoader();
    function loadCosmosTexture(file: string): THREE.Texture {
      const tex = textureLoader.load(
        `${cosmosAssetBase}textures/cosmos/${file}`,
        undefined,
        undefined,
        (err: unknown) => console.error(LOG, `Failed to load texture ${file}`, err),
      );
      tex.colorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace;
      return tex;
    }

    const accentColor = readVarColor("--color-accent", "#7c8aff");
    const mutedColor = readVarColor("--color-muted", "#8b8e96");

    // Dark is the site's only theme now, so these never get retinted live
    // after construction - kept as plain Color instances (not baked
    // constants) purely so every material below can share one read of the
    // accent/muted tokens rather than re-parsing the CSS custom property
    // per material.
    const accentTargets: THREE.Color[] = [];
    const mutedTargets: THREE.Color[] = [];

    // Layered starfield: runs on every device (tiered down on mobile/low
    // power via starfieldProfile), unlike the six-object scene below which
    // stays desktop-only for this phase. Added directly to the scene, not
    // the anchor, so it stays centered on the viewer regardless of where
    // the anchor shifts for the six-object layout.
    const starfield = createStarfield(starfieldProfile, accentColor, reduceMotion);
    scene.add(starfield.group);

    // Meteors and the occasional supernova. On the sky rather than on any
    // waypoint, so they play on every page - and on every tier, unlike the
    // six-object scene: a phone gets the starfield and nothing else, which
    // makes it the tier with the most to gain from something happening in
    // it. The cost is one draw call and a sprite.
    const skyEvents = createSkyEvents(accentColor, reduceMotion);
    scene.add(skyEvents.object);

    // Shared by the galaxy's shear and the nebula's drift (pointMotion.ts):
    // both are vertex-shader effects driven by elapsed seconds, and one
    // uniform object handed to both materials means one number to advance
    // per frame instead of a uniform update per material.
    const motionClock = createMotionClock();
    // Radians per second at the disc's centre, falling off with radius.
    // A full turn of the core takes about six minutes.
    const GALAXY_SHEAR = 0.006;
    // Local units. The cloud is a few units across, so this is a drift of
    // well under a percent - gas moving, not the nebula wobbling.
    const NEBULA_DRIFT = 0.012;

    // Created only alongside the six-object scene: it exists to sell travel
    // between waypoints, and mobile has neither the objects nor the journey.
    let transitDust: TransitDust | null = null;

    function makeGlowSprite(scale: number, opacity: number, initialColor = accentColor, track: THREE.Color[] | null = accentTargets): THREE.Sprite {
      const mat = new THREE.SpriteMaterial({
        map: glowTexture,
        color: initialColor,
        transparent: true,
        opacity,
        blending: THREE.AdditiveBlending,
        depthWrite: false,
      });
      const sprite = new THREE.Sprite(mat);
      sprite.scale.set(scale, scale, 1);
      if (track) track.push(mat.color);
      return sprite;
    }

    // Anchor: repositioned on resize so the whole cosmos leans right of
    // center (menu occupies the left column), the same screen-fraction
    // trick HomeEmblem used for the atom, generalized to a wider system.
    // Declared unconditionally (resize() always positions it) even though
    // it stays empty on mobile - cheap, and avoids threading isDesktop
    // through resize() for no real benefit.
    const anchor = new THREE.Group();
    scene.add(anchor);

    type CosmosKey = "about" | "projects" | "playground" | "music" | "photography" | "contact";
    const objects: Partial<Record<CosmosKey, THREE.Group>> = {};

    // ---- Spatial layout ----
    // Previously all six objects shared an ~8-unit-deep shell viewed from
    // ~10 units away, every one scaled into the same on-screen envelope.
    // That's why it read as a diagram of space rather than space: a galaxy,
    // a star and a hand-sized probe all had the same visual weight.
    //
    // Now each object is built at its own extent and parked at its own
    // depth, and everything *not* being visited is left at its true angular
    // size from wherever you happen to be standing - which is usually a
    // speck, or nothing at all. That's what makes the vista read as deep.
    //
    // `scale` maps the group's locally-authored size (all are roughly 1.5
    // units across as built) onto that extent. It is *not* a physically
    // honest ratio and can't be: Voyager to the Milky Way is a factor of
    // 10^20, which no renderer represents. What it preserves is the
    // ordering and a sense of enormous range (~170x here) while keeping
    // every object far enough from the near plane to actually draw.
    //
    // `frame` is the fraction of the viewport each object should occupy
    // once you arrive - the camera's parking distance is derived from it
    // and the object's *measured* extent (see measureExtents below) rather
    // than hand-tuned. Hand-tuning was the bug: it silently drifted out of
    // agreement with the geometry, leaving the pulsar at 10% of frame
    // height and the probe at 3% - which is to say, invisible.
    //
    // `offset` is an x/y nudge in multiples of that same parking distance,
    // so the flight path weaves instead of running straight down the z
    // axis. An object's own offset doesn't change its framing (the camera
    // centres on it either way); it only moves where its neighbours sit.
    //
    // Depth is *not* listed here. It used to be, as six hand-picked z
    // values, and that was the cause of the journey's worst moment: the
    // gap from About to Contact was 1000 units of travel toward something
    // meant to be viewed from three, so for most of that leg the
    // destination was a fraction of a pixel and the screen was empty. You
    // could be mid-journey with nothing on screen at all and no way to tell
    // where you were. Spacing is derived from the objects instead - see
    // waypointDepth() below.
    const OBJECT_LAYOUT: Record<
      CosmosKey,
      { offset: [number, number]; scale: number; frame: number }
    > = {
      // `frame` values above 1 are deliberate for the large diffuse
      // objects. A nebula or a galaxy has no edge to respect - the extent
      // measured off its bounding box includes the faint outer wisps, so
      // fitting the whole box into the viewport frames a great deal of
      // nothing around a small bright middle. Letting them run past the
      // edges is how they are actually looked at, and it is what puts them
      // in the same space as the page's text rather than beside it.
      playground:  { offset: [0.25, 0.10],   scale: 37.5,  frame: 1.20 }, // nebula
      projects:    { offset: [-0.20, 0.15],  scale: 8.3,   frame: 0.80 }, // planetary system
      photography: { offset: [0.15, -0.20],  scale: 187.5, frame: 1.14 }, // galaxy
      music:       { offset: [-0.40, -0.14], scale: 2.6,   frame: 1.02 }, // pulsar
      about:       { offset: [0.20, 0.25],   scale: 2.14,  frame: 1.10 }, // star
      // Over 1 on purpose, and the only one that is. Framing is fitted to
      // an object's bounding box, which assumes the box is a fair proxy for
      // what you look at - true for a nebula or a star, false for a
      // spacecraft with a magnetometer boom several times longer than the
      // body it hangs off. Fitting that box put the whole probe at about a
      // quarter of the frame. Letting the boom run past the edges is both
      // better looking and closer to how the thing is actually photographed.
      contact:     { offset: [-0.17, 0.22],  scale: 1.1,   frame: 1.70 }, // probe
    };

    // Built at the origin's x/y and moved into place by placeObjects() once
    // every object has been measured - position doesn't affect an object's
    // own framing, so it can wait until the parking distances are known.
    function placeObject(group: THREE.Group, key: CosmosKey) {
      group.position.set(0, 0, 0);
      group.scale.setScalar(OBJECT_LAYOUT[key].scale);
    }

    // The six narrative objects are part of the desktop game-menu
    // experience (see HomeMenu.astro) and stay desktop-only for this
    // phase - foundation work here is the starfield above, not a mobile
    // redesign of the menu scene.
    // Set once the framing block below has finished initialising. Model
    // loads call refreshFraming() from their onLoad, and an onLoad can
    // land *during* this module's own evaluation when the file comes
    // straight from cache - the script suspends on its dynamic imports
    // and the callback runs in the gap. Everything refreshFraming touches
    // is declared further down, so that call hits the temporal dead zone
    // and throws; GLTFLoader catches it, reports it as a load failure, and
    // the model is silently never added. Which is a bug that only appears
    // on machines fast enough to win the race - a warm cache locally, and
    // never in a cold-cache test run.
    //
    // Skipping the refresh in that window is safe: a model that arrives
    // that early is already in the scene graph when the initial measuring
    // pass runs, so it is measured anyway.
    let framingReady = false;

    if (isDesktop) {
    // Marks that the six narrative objects actually exist behind the page,
    // which the scrim rule above needs to distinguish from a starfield-only
    // tier. Set here rather than from the breakpoint so it stays true for
    // the life of the tab, exactly like the decision it records.
    container.dataset.cosmosObjects = "true";
    transitDust = createTransitDust(200, accentColor);
    scene.add(transitDust.object);

    const ambientLight = new THREE.AmbientLight(0xffffff, 0.7);
    scene.add(ambientLight);

    // Directional, not point, lights. A point light's inverse-square
    // falloff was fine when every object sat within ~8 units of the anchor,
    // but the objects now span 8000 units of depth and are scaled by up to
    // 187x - any point light bright enough for the probe would blow out the
    // nebula, and any light tuned for the nebula leaves everything past it
    // black. Directional light has neither distance nor scale dependence
    // (only direction), so one key/rim pair lights all six identically.
    const keyLight = new THREE.DirectionalLight(0xffffff, 2.6);
    keyLight.position.set(4, 5, 8);
    scene.add(keyLight);
    const rimLight = new THREE.DirectionalLight(0xffffff, 1.1);
    rimLight.position.set(-5, -3, -2);
    scene.add(rimLight);

    // ---- About: the star ----
    {
      const group = new THREE.Group();
      placeObject(group, "about");
      anchor.add(group);

      // A star is its own light source, so the Sun texture drives emissive
      // as well as diffuse - it reads correctly no matter where the scene
      // lights point, and can't go dark now that it sits 7000 units from
      // the lighting rig's nominal origin.
      // Unlit on purpose. A star is its own light source - it has no
      // terminator and no shaded side, so every one of MeshStandardMaterial's
      // per-fragment lighting terms was being evaluated to produce something
      // indistinguishable from the texture itself. That was invisible when
      // the Sun was a small ball; now that it fills a third of the screen
      // it was the single most expensive surface in the scene. Brightness
      // rides on `color` instead of emissiveIntensity.
      const sunTexture = loadCosmosTexture("sun-2k.jpg");
      const starMat = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ map: sunTexture, color: 0xffffff });
      // A real sphere rather than a subdivided icosahedron: at this framing
      // the star fills a third of the screen and the icosahedron's facets
      // showed on the limb as a visibly polygonal edge.
      const star = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.SphereGeometry(0.5, 64, 48), starMat);
      group.add(star);

      const coronaInner = makeGlowSprite(1.6, 0.55);
      const coronaOuter = makeGlowSprite(2.8, 0.28);
      group.add(coronaInner, coronaOuter);

      // A mass ejection, every minute or two.
      //
      // The star was the object with the least happening on it: a texture,
      // a slow scale pulse and two breathing coronae, all of which are
      // loops. This is the one thing it does that has a beginning.
      //
      // Built as a short line of sprites rather than one stretched sprite
      // because a sprite cannot be stretched along a direction in the
      // world - it is billboarded, and its only rotation control is in
      // screen space. Four overlapping puffs of falling size, oriented as
      // a group, read as a plume from any angle the camera can reach.
      const flare = new THREE.Group();
      flare.visible = false;
      group.add(flare);
      const FLARE_PUFFS = 4;
      const flarePuffs: THREE.Sprite[] = [];
      const flareHot = new THREE.Color(0xffe6b8);
      const flareCool = new THREE.Color(0xff7a2e);
      for (let i = 0; i < FLARE_PUFFS; i++) {
        const t = i / (FLARE_PUFFS - 1);
        // Sized and spaced against the coronae, not against the star. The
        // inner corona is a 1.6-unit glow sprite over a 0.5-unit star, so
        // anything that stays close to the surface is simply swallowed by
        // it - the first version was invisible for exactly that reason.
        const puff = makeGlowSprite(0.6 * (1 - t * 0.4), 0, flareHot.clone().lerp(flareCool, t), null);
        puff.position.y = t * 0.85;
        flare.add(puff);
        flarePuffs.push(puff);
      }

      group.userData = { starMat, star, coronaInner, coronaOuter, flare, flarePuffs };
      objects.about = group;
    }

    // ---- Projects: the solar system ----
    // Built from published orbital elements rather than eyeballed rings.
    // What was here before was three tinted spheres on three separately
    // tilted hoops with a scanned asteroid standing in at Mercury's orbit,
    // and it read exactly as what it was: a diagram of a solar system by
    // someone who hadn't looked one up.
    //
    // Two quantities have to be compressed, and they can't both be honest.
    // Jupiter's radius is 1/85,000th of Pluto's orbit, so *any* drawing of
    // this at any size already misstates size against distance by four
    // orders of magnitude. The only real question is how that unavoidable
    // lie is distributed, so it's spent deliberately, in two places, and
    // nowhere else:
    //
    //   orbit radius - logarithmic in the true semi-major axis. Keeps the
    //     true ordering and the true shape of the spacing; Jupiter still
    //     opens by far the widest gap in the system, because it really does.
    //   body radius  - true radius to the power 0.6. Sizing the bodies
    //     truly against each other puts Mercury at 2px and Pluto under 1px
    //     beside a 40px Jupiter: correct, and unreadable. The 0.6 exponent
    //     keeps Jupiter unmistakably the giant (4x Earth here against 11x
    //     in life) while leaving every body big enough to show its own face.
    //
    // Everything else is real and uncompressed: eccentricity, inclination,
    // ascending node, argument of perihelion, axial tilt, spin direction,
    // ring geometry, and where all eleven bodies are standing right now.
    {
      const SYSTEM_TILT = 0.44;
      const group = new THREE.Group();
      placeObject(group, "projects");
      // Tip the ecliptic toward the viewer. Everything below builds the
      // system in its own plane, which puts the plane exactly edge-on to a
      // camera looking down -Z - nine planets and their orbits collapsed
      // onto one horizontal line, which is both unreadable and, since
      // Saturn's rings vanish to a stroke at that angle, a waste of the one
      // fix this waypoint most needed. 25 degrees is enough to open the
      // orbits into ellipses and show the rings as rings.
      //
      // Named because makeOrbitRing() needs it too: it has to know which way
      // the camera is looking in this group's frame to turn its ribbons
      // toward it, and that is exactly this angle undone.
      group.rotation.x = SYSTEM_TILT;
      anchor.add(group);

      const DEG = Math.PI / 180;

      const ORBIT_INNER = 0.34; // Mercury
      const ORBIT_OUTER = 1.6; // Pluto
      const LOG_INNER = Math.log(0.3871);
      const LOG_SPAN = Math.log(39.4817) - LOG_INNER;
      function orbitRadius(au: number): number {
        return Math.max(0.05, ORBIT_INNER + ((Math.log(au) - LOG_INNER) / LOG_SPAN) * (ORBIT_OUTER - ORBIT_INNER));
      }

      const JUPITER_KM = 69911;
      const JUPITER_DRAWN = 0.105;
      function bodyRadius(km: number): number {
        return JUPITER_DRAWN * Math.pow(km / JUPITER_KM, 0.6);
      }

      // Kepler's third law, applied to the radii actually drawn instead of
      // the real ones. Real period ratios across a compressed system leave
      // the outer half of it frozen - 248 years for Pluto against 88 days
      // for Mercury - so the drawn system instead obeys the law its own
      // geometry implies. Closer is still faster, still by exactly a^-3/2,
      // just over a shorter range.
      const INNER_PERIOD_S = 14;
      function meanMotion(a: number): number {
        return (Math.PI * 2) / (INNER_PERIOD_S * Math.pow(a / ORBIT_INNER, 1.5));
      }

      // Spin gets the same treatment for the same reason: at true ratios an
      // Earth day would pass in 0.09s and Venus would need 16 minutes.
      const EARTH_DAY_S = 4;
      function spinRate(hours: number): number {
        return (Math.PI * 2) / (EARTH_DAY_S * (hours / 23.934));
      }

      // Mean anomaly is the J2000 value from JPL's fact sheets, wound
      // forward to now at each body's *real* period. So the arrangement you
      // arrive on is the arrangement outside, today - Jupiter is where
      // Jupiter is. From that instant it runs on drawn time and the
      // agreement decays; it's a starting condition, not a claim.
      const daysSinceJ2000 = (Date.now() - Date.UTC(2000, 0, 1, 12)) / 86400000;

      // Perifocal basis: P points at perihelion, Q is a quarter turn ahead
      // in the orbit plane, and a position is just a(cosE - e)*P +
      // a*sqrt(1-e^2)*sinE*Q. Cheaper than three nested rotation nodes per
      // body and, more to the point, it's one expression used by both the
      // per-frame position and the orbit line, so the drawn ellipse cannot
      // drift out of agreement with the body travelling along it.
      // Ecliptic coordinates come out z-north and are handed to three.js
      // y-up on the way: (x, y, z) -> (x, z, -y).
      function perifocal(inc: number, node: number, peri: number) {
        const ci = Math.cos(inc * DEG), si = Math.sin(inc * DEG);
        const cO = Math.cos(node * DEG), sO = Math.sin(node * DEG);
        const cw = Math.cos(peri * DEG), sw = Math.sin(peri * DEG);
        return {
          P: new THREE.Vector3(cO * cw - sO * sw * ci, sw * si, -(sO * cw + cO * sw * ci)),
          Q: new THREE.Vector3(-cO * sw - sO * cw * ci, cw * si, -(-sO * sw + cO * cw * ci)),
        };
      }

      // M = E - e*sinE, by Newton. Seeded the standard way for high
      // eccentricity and step-capped, because Halley's e is 0.967 and the
      // textbook E0 = M start diverges outright up there.
      function eccentricAnomaly(m: number, e: number): number {
        let E = m + 0.85 * e * Math.sign(Math.sin(m) || 1);
        for (let k = 0; k < 24; k++) {
          const f = E - e * Math.sin(E) - m;
          if (Math.abs(f) < 1e-9) break;
          E -= THREE.MathUtils.clamp(f / Math.max(1 - e * Math.cos(E), 1e-4), -1, 1);
        }
        return E;
      }

      type Orbit = { au: number; e: number; bau: number; P: THREE.Vector3; Q: THREE.Vector3; n: number; m: number };
      function makeOrbit(au: number, e: number, inc: number, node: number, peri: number, m0: number, periodDays: number): Orbit {
        const { P, Q } = perifocal(inc, node, peri);
        return {
          au,
          e,
          bau: au * Math.sqrt(1 - e * e),
          P,
          Q,
          n: meanMotion(orbitRadius(au)),
          m: (m0 * DEG + (Math.PI * 2 * daysSinceJ2000) / periodDays) % (Math.PI * 2),
        };
      }

      // The true position, in AU, pulled in along its own radius by the same
      // logarithmic compression the orbit radii use.
      //
      // Compressing the semi-major axis and then drawing a true-shaped
      // ellipse around it - the obvious way, and the first way this was
      // written - does not survive eccentricity, because the compression is
      // nonlinear and an ellipse's shape is not. It put Halley's perihelion
      // at 0.045 units, which is to say inside the drawn Sun, and Pluto's
      // 20% deeper under Neptune than it really goes. Compressing the radius
      // instead keeps every body's distance from the Sun honest at every
      // point of its orbit, and its direction exactly right; the price is
      // that the drawn path is a warped ellipse rather than an exact one.
      // Between the shape of the curve and the distances along it, the
      // distances are the thing worth getting right.
      function orbitPoint(o: Orbit, E: number, out: THREE.Vector3): THREE.Vector3 {
        out
          .copy(o.P)
          .multiplyScalar(o.au * (Math.cos(E) - o.e))
          .addScaledVector(o.Q, o.bau * Math.sin(E));
        const r = out.length();
        return r > 1e-9 ? out.multiplyScalar(orbitRadius(r) / r) : out;
      }

      // One faint ellipse per body, sampled from the same orbitPoint() the
      // body itself is positioned by, so the drawn path cannot drift out of
      // agreement with the body travelling along it.
      //
      // Built as a triangle ribbon rather than the obvious THREE.Line.
      // gl.LINE_STRIP is the least dependable primitive in WebGL: width is
      // clamped to 1px by every modern driver regardless of what you ask
      // for, which on a hidpi display is a half-density hairline, and strips
      // are the one primitive drivers are known to disagree about - they can
      // come back partially drawn, which reads as orbits that stop in mid
      // air rather than close. A ribbon is triangles, and triangles are the
      // thing every GPU is actually built to rasterize identically.
      //
      // Drawn as a tube along the path, which is the one construction that
      // looks the same from wherever you happen to be standing.
      //
      // Two earlier attempts failed on the same point. A flat ribbon lying
      // in the orbital plane is what a real orbit is, but the plane is tipped
      // only 25 degrees off edge-on, so the ribbon is viewed nearly edge-on
      // too: its width foreshortens, and anywhere that pushes it under a
      // pixel the rasterizer covers no sample and draws nothing at all. Not
      // a fainter line - no line. Turning the ribbon to face the camera fixes
      // that only while the camera is where you assumed it was, and it is
      // not: clicking a waypoint dives to a third of the parked distance,
      // which for this object is *inside* the shell of orbits. Seen from in
      // there the assumed view direction is wrong by a lot and differently
      // wrong across the frame, so the ribbons flare into broad flat bands
      // in some places and vanish in others.
      //
      // A tube has no preferred viewing direction to get wrong. It costs
      // about 2,900 triangles per orbit, which against everything else in
      // this scene is nothing, and it removes the entire class of problem
      // rather than the instance of it.
      const ORBIT_COLOR = new THREE.Color(0xb9c6dd);
      const ORBIT_RADIUS = 0.0038;
      function makeOrbitRing(o: Orbit, color: THREE.Color, opacity: number): THREE.Mesh {
        const segments = 240;
        const path: THREE.Vector3[] = [];
        for (let i = 0; i < segments; i++) {
          path.push(orbitPoint(o, (i / segments) * Math.PI * 2, new THREE.Vector3()));
        }
        // Tension 0 (centripetal would round the corners of a closed loop
        // that has none). Closed, so the seam is interpolated like any other
        // sample and leaves no notch where the ellipse meets itself.
        const curve = new THREE.CatmullRomCurve3(path, true, "catmullrom", 0);
        return new THREE.Mesh(
          new THREE.TubeGeometry(curve, segments, ORBIT_RADIUS, 6, true),
          new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({
            color,
            transparent: true,
            opacity,
            side: THREE.DoubleSide,
            depthWrite: false,
            ...ADDITIVE_WITH_ALPHA,
          }),
        );
      }

      // The Sun. Same texture as the About waypoint's star, which is the
      // point: arriving at Projects and finding the object from About at the
      // centre of it is how the two waypoints admit they are the same kind
      // of thing seen from different distances.
      //
      // Drawn to neither of this scene's two rules and there is no honest
      // way around that. True to the orbits it would be 0.004 units across,
      // smaller than Mercury; true to the size curve it would be 0.42, wider
      // than Mercury's whole orbit. Both are absurd, so this is a chosen
      // number, and it is the only one in the block.
      const SUN_DRAWN = 0.13;
      const sunMat = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ map: loadCosmosTexture("sun-2k.jpg") });
      const sun = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.SphereGeometry(SUN_DRAWN, 48, 32), sunMat);
      group.add(sun);
      // Kept tight. Mercury's orbit is only 2.6 solar radii out at this
      // compression, so a corona sized like the one at the About waypoint -
      // where the star is alone and can afford to bloom - reaches past the
      // four inner planets and washes them out of their own system.
      const sunGlow = makeGlowSprite(SUN_DRAWN * 3.2, 0.5, new THREE.Color(0xffe7bd), null);
      const sunHalo = makeGlowSprite(SUN_DRAWN * 6.5, 0.15, new THREE.Color(0xffd9a0), null);
      group.add(sunGlow, sunHalo);

      // Illumination from the system's own star, which the scene did not
      // have before: the shared key/rim pair is directional, so every planet
      // was lit from the same corner of the sky no matter where its sun
      // was, and the phases were nonsense.
      //
      // decay 0 is deliberate. True inverse-square across 0.39 to 39 AU is
      // 10,000:1 - physically exact, and it would leave everything past
      // Jupiter unlit. The distance cap is what keeps this local: the next
      // nearest object in the scene is thousands of units away, so nothing
      // else can be touched by it.
      const sunLight = new THREE.PointLight(0xfff2e0, 4, 34, 0);
      group.add(sunLight);

      type Ring = { inner: number; outer: number; texture?: string; opacity: number };
      type Body = {
        name: string;
        texture: string;
        tint: number;
        km: number;
        obliquity: number; // degrees from the orbit normal
        dayHours: number; // sidereal, negative = retrograde spin
        au: number;
        e: number;
        inc: number; // degrees to the ecliptic
        node: number; // longitude of ascending node
        peri: number; // argument of perihelion
        m0: number; // mean anomaly at J2000
        periodDays: number;
        ring?: Ring; // radii in planet radii
      };

      // J2000 elements from JPL's planetary fact sheets; radii, obliquities
      // and rotation periods likewise. Venus, Uranus and Pluto spin
      // backwards and say so in the sign.
      const BODIES: Body[] = [
        { name: "Mercury", texture: "mercury-1k.jpg", tint: 0xffffff, km: 2440, obliquity: 0.03, dayHours: 1407.6,
          au: 0.38710, e: 0.20563, inc: 7.005, node: 48.331, peri: 29.125, m0: 174.796, periodDays: 87.969 },
        { name: "Venus", texture: "venus-1k.jpg", tint: 0xffffff, km: 6052, obliquity: 177.36, dayHours: -5832.5,
          au: 0.72333, e: 0.00677, inc: 3.395, node: 76.680, peri: 54.884, m0: 50.115, periodDays: 224.701 },
        { name: "Earth", texture: "earth-1k.jpg", tint: 0xffffff, km: 6371, obliquity: 23.44, dayHours: 23.934,
          au: 1.00000, e: 0.01671, inc: 0.000, node: -11.261, peri: 114.208, m0: 358.617, periodDays: 365.256 },
        { name: "Mars", texture: "mars-1k.jpg", tint: 0xffffff, km: 3390, obliquity: 25.19, dayHours: 24.623,
          au: 1.52368, e: 0.09340, inc: 1.850, node: 49.558, peri: 286.502, m0: 19.373, periodDays: 686.980 },
        { name: "Jupiter", texture: "jupiter-1k.jpg", tint: 0xffffff, km: 69911, obliquity: 3.13, dayHours: 9.925,
          au: 5.20260, e: 0.04849, inc: 1.303, node: 100.464, peri: 273.867, m0: 20.020, periodDays: 4332.589 },
        // Ring radii are the real C-through-A span, 1.24 to 2.27 Saturn
        // radii, which is what the texture strip covers.
        { name: "Saturn", texture: "saturn-1k.jpg", tint: 0xffffff, km: 58232, obliquity: 26.73, dayHours: 10.656,
          au: 9.55491, e: 0.05551, inc: 2.485, node: 113.665, peri: 339.392, m0: 317.020, periodDays: 10759.22,
          ring: { inner: 1.24, outer: 2.27, texture: "saturn-ring-2k.png", opacity: 0.95 } },
        // Uranus has rings too - narrow, dark, and standing almost straight
        // up because the planet is knocked over 98 degrees. Untextured and
        // barely there on purpose: that is how they are.
        { name: "Uranus", texture: "uranus-1k.jpg", tint: 0xffffff, km: 25362, obliquity: 97.77, dayHours: -17.24,
          au: 19.21845, e: 0.04630, inc: 0.773, node: 74.006, peri: 96.999, m0: 142.239, periodDays: 30685.4,
          ring: { inner: 1.64, outer: 2.0, opacity: 0.18 } },
        { name: "Neptune", texture: "neptune-1k.jpg", tint: 0xffffff, km: 24622, obliquity: 28.32, dayHours: 16.11,
          au: 30.11039, e: 0.00899, inc: 1.770, node: 131.784, peri: 276.336, m0: 256.228, periodDays: 60189 },
        // The greyscale New Horizons albedo map times Pluto's mean colour -
        // see the note in public/textures/cosmos/License.txt. The 17-degree
        // inclination and 0.249 eccentricity are the reason Pluto is worth
        // drawing: it leans out of the plane everything else lies in, and
        // its perihelion falls inside Neptune's orbit. Both are visible here.
        { name: "Pluto", texture: "pluto-1k.jpg", tint: 0xc9a888, km: 1188, obliquity: 122.53, dayHours: -153.29,
          au: 39.48169, e: 0.24883, inc: 17.16, node: 110.299, peri: 113.834, m0: 14.53, periodDays: 90560 },
      ];

      // RingGeometry's default UVs run along vertex position, not polar
      // radius, so a ring texture that encodes inner-to-outer banding as a
      // thin strip - which is exactly what Saturn's is - samples wrong out
      // of the box. Remap so U tracks the 0..1 fraction from inner to outer.
      function remapRingUVs(geometry: THREE.RingGeometry, innerRadius: number, outerRadius: number) {
        const pos = geometry.attributes.position;
        const uv = geometry.attributes.uv;
        const v3 = new THREE.Vector3();
        for (let i = 0; i < pos.count; i++) {
          v3.fromBufferAttribute(pos, i);
          uv.setXY(i, THREE.MathUtils.clamp((v3.length() - innerRadius) / (outerRadius - innerRadius), 0, 1), 0.5);
        }
        uv.needsUpdate = true;
      }

      const sphereGeo = new THREE.SphereGeometry(1, 32, 24);
      const planets: { orbit: Orbit; holder: THREE.Object3D; spinner: THREE.Object3D; spin: number }[] = [];
      const orbitLineMats: THREE.MeshBasicMaterial[] = [];

      BODIES.forEach((body) => {
        const orbit = makeOrbit(body.au, body.e, body.inc, body.node, body.peri, body.m0, body.periodDays);
        const radius = bodyRadius(body.km);

        const holder = new THREE.Object3D();
        group.add(holder);

        // Axial tilt lives on its own node between the orbit and the body,
        // so the planet spins about its own pole rather than the orbit
        // normal - and so anything in the planet's equatorial plane can hang
        // off the same node and inherit the tilt for free. Which is the
        // whole fix for Saturn: its rings used to be a separate rotation
        // bolted onto a separately tilted orbit hoop, and wobbled around at
        // an angle that belonged to neither the planet nor the ecliptic.
        // They are in the equatorial plane now, because that is where they
        // are.
        const tilt = new THREE.Object3D();
        tilt.rotation.z = body.obliquity * DEG;
        holder.add(tilt);

        const mat = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({
          map: loadCosmosTexture(body.texture),
          color: body.tint,
          roughness: 0.85,
          metalness: 0,
        });
        const mesh = new THREE.Mesh(sphereGeo, mat);
        mesh.scale.setScalar(radius);
        tilt.add(mesh);

        if (body.ring) {
          const inner = radius * body.ring.inner;
          const outer = radius * body.ring.outer;
          const ringGeo = new THREE.RingGeometry(inner, outer, 96);
          remapRingUVs(ringGeo, inner, outer);
          const ring = new THREE.Mesh(
            ringGeo,
            new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({
              map: body.ring.texture ? loadCosmosTexture(body.ring.texture) : null,
              color: 0xffffff,
              transparent: true,
              opacity: body.ring.opacity,
              side: THREE.DoubleSide,
              depthWrite: false,
            }),
          );
          // RingGeometry is built in the XY plane; the equator is XZ.
          ring.rotation.x = -Math.PI / 2;
          tilt.add(ring);
        }

        const line = makeOrbitRing(orbit, ORBIT_COLOR, 0.9);
        group.add(line);
        orbitLineMats.push(line.material as THREE.MeshBasicMaterial);

        planets.push({ orbit, holder, spinner: mesh, spin: spinRate(body.dayHours) });
      });

      // Itokawa, on Itokawa's actual orbit. It used to sit at the innermost
      // slot standing in for a planet, where a 500-metre rubble pile read as
      // a deformed Mercury - the one thing in the scene that was real
      // geometry, and it was the thing that looked fake. Its real orbit
      // crosses Earth's, which is both more interesting and less work.
      const itokawaOrbit = makeOrbit(1.324163, 0.28011, 1.6214, 69.081, 162.820, 294.3, 556.4);
      const itokawaHolder = new THREE.Object3D();
      group.add(itokawaHolder);
      group.add(makeOrbitRing(itokawaOrbit, ORBIT_COLOR, 0.5));
      gltfLoader.load(
        `${cosmosAssetBase}models/cosmos/itokawa.glb`,
        (gltf: any) => {
          const mesh = gltf.scene;
          const box = new THREE.Box3().setFromObject(mesh);
          const size = box.getSize(new THREE.Vector3()).length();
          // Real relative size would be a third of a pixel. Drawn at roughly
          // Pluto's size so the shape - which is the entire reason it is
          // here rather than another sphere - can be seen at all.
          mesh.scale.setScalar(0.02 / (size || 1));
          // NASA's shape-model export carries a bare white metallic PBR
          // material with no environment map to reflect, so it renders
          // near-black under the scene's lights alone. Overridden with a
          // matte rock tint so the real scanned geometry actually reads.
          mesh.traverse((child: any) => {
            if (child.isMesh) {
              child.material = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x8a7c6e, roughness: 0.95, metalness: 0 });
            }
          });
          itokawaHolder.add(mesh);
          itokawaHolder.userData.mesh = mesh;
          refreshFraming("projects");
        },
        undefined,
        (err: unknown) => console.error(LOG, "Failed to load asteroid model", err),
      );

      // ---- 1P/Halley ----
      // Real elements, and they are the whole joke: e = 0.967 and an
      // inclination of 162 degrees, so it goes round the Sun *backwards*,
      // against the direction all nine of the others travel, on a path tipped
      // most of the way over from the plane they share. Aphelion falls just
      // inside Pluto's orbit and it spends almost the entire lap out there
      // barely moving - Kepler's second law is unkind to comets - then drops
      // through the middle of the system in a few seconds with both tails up
      // and climbs back out.
      //
      // No orbit line, unlike every other body here. A permanently drawn
      // path would announce it in advance, and the thing about a comet is
      // that it shows up.
      const halleyOrbit = makeOrbit(17.834, 0.96714, 162.262, 58.42, 111.33, 66.4, 27512);
      const halley = new THREE.Object3D();
      // Kept out of the framing fit - see expandExtent(). A 162-degree
      // inclination throws it far enough out of the shared plane that
      // fitting the frame around it would shrink the entire solar system to
      // leave room for one body that is invisible most of the time anyway.
      halley.userData.noExtent = true;
      group.add(halley);

      const comaMat = new THREE.SpriteMaterial({
        map: glowTexture,
        color: new THREE.Color(0xdff0ff),
        transparent: true,
        opacity: 0,
        depthWrite: false,
        ...ADDITIVE_WITH_ALPHA,
      });
      const coma = new THREE.Sprite(comaMat);
      coma.scale.setScalar(0.05);
      halley.add(coma);

      // Two tails, because a comet has two and they point in different
      // directions - which is the detail that makes one read as a comet
      // rather than a smear. The ion tail is gas, blown dead anti-sunward by
      // the solar wind, narrow and blue. The dust tail is heavier, keeps
      // some of the comet's own orbital motion, and so lags behind along the
      // path and spreads wider, and is warmer because it is lit by reflected
      // sunlight rather than glowing on its own.
      //
      // Built from a chain of overlapping soft sprites rather than the
      // obvious tapered cone. A cone is a shell: every vertex is on the
      // silhouette, so there is no interior to make brighter than the edge,
      // and it renders as a hard-edged triangular wedge with a crisp
      // boundary against space. A comet tail has no boundary. Sprites are
      // soft in both directions at once and cost nothing at this count.
      type Tail = { holder: THREE.Object3D; mats: THREE.SpriteMaterial[]; weights: number[] };
      function makeTail(count: number, length: number, spread: number, color: number, falloff: number): Tail {
        const holder = new THREE.Object3D();
        const mats: THREE.SpriteMaterial[] = [];
        const weights: number[] = [];
        for (let i = 0; i < count; i++) {
          const t = (i + 0.5) / count;
          const mat = new THREE.SpriteMaterial({
            map: glowTexture,
            color: new THREE.Color(color),
            transparent: true,
            opacity: 0,
            depthWrite: false,
            ...ADDITIVE_WITH_ALPHA,
          });
          const sprite = new THREE.Sprite(mat);
          // Hangs off the nucleus down its own -Y; the holder is what gets
          // pointed anti-sunward each frame.
          sprite.position.y = -t * length;
          // Each sprite has to be several times wider than the gap to the
          // next one or the chain reads as a dotted line rather than a
          // plume - the glow texture falls off well before its own edge, so
          // "touching" is not enough, they have to genuinely overlap.
          sprite.scale.setScalar(0.05 + t * spread);
          holder.add(sprite);
          mats.push(mat);
          weights.push(Math.pow(1 - t, falloff));
        }
        return { holder, mats, weights };
      }
      function setTailStrength(tail: Tail, strength: number) {
        for (let i = 0; i < tail.mats.length; i++) tail.mats[i].opacity = strength * tail.weights[i];
      }
      const ionTail = makeTail(44, 0.78, 0.055, 0xa8d4ff, 1.4);
      const dustTail = makeTail(30, 0.46, 0.12, 0xffd9a8, 1.9);
      halley.add(ionTail.holder, dustTail.holder);

      const prevHalley = new THREE.Vector3();
      const sunward = new THREE.Vector3();
      const trailing = new THREE.Vector3();
      const TAIL_AXIS = new THREE.Vector3(0, -1, 0);
      let halleySeeded = false;

      function advance(o: Orbit, dt: number): number {
        o.m = (o.m + o.n * dt) % (Math.PI * 2);
        return eccentricAnomaly(o.m, o.e);
      }

      group.userData = {
        update(dt: number, dim: number) {
          sun.rotation.y += spinRate(609.12) * dt; // the Sun turns once in ~25.4 days
          sunGlow.material.opacity = 0.5 * dim;
          sunHalo.material.opacity = 0.15 * dim;
          orbitLineMats.forEach((m) => { m.opacity = 0.9 * dim; });

          planets.forEach((p) => {
            orbitPoint(p.orbit, advance(p.orbit, dt), p.holder.position);
            p.spinner.rotation.y += p.spin * dt;
          });

          orbitPoint(itokawaOrbit, advance(itokawaOrbit, dt), itokawaHolder.position);
          const rock = itokawaHolder.userData.mesh as THREE.Object3D | undefined;
          if (rock) {
            // A tumble on two axes rather than a spin on one. Itokawa is a
            // contact binary held together by its own weak gravity; it has
            // no settled pole to spin about.
            rock.rotation.x += 0.5 * dt;
            rock.rotation.y += 0.8 * dt;
          }

          prevHalley.copy(halley.position);
          orbitPoint(halleyOrbit, advance(halleyOrbit, dt), halley.position);
          if (!halleySeeded) {
            prevHalley.copy(halley.position);
            halleySeeded = true;
          }

          // Two thresholds rather than one, matching what is actually
          // observable. On the way in it is a bare point of light, and that
          // is all it is for most of the run. The tails only grow once it is
          // inside roughly Jupiter's distance, which is about where there is
          // enough sunlight to boil ice off it, and they are at full length
          // for a few seconds around perihelion - which for Halley falls
          // between Mercury's orbit and Venus's.
          //
          // The tail window is short and there is no honest way to make it
          // long: e = 0.967 means Kepler's second law spends the lap out at
          // aphelion and crosses the whole inner system in a few seconds.
          // So the coma carries the visible run instead - a bright point
          // sweeping in from past Neptune and back out over about 25 of the
          // 115 seconds - and the tails are the few seconds in the middle of
          // it that are worth catching. 7.6 AU for tail onset is late enough
          // to be true (Halley was recovered showing activity further out
          // than that in 1982) and early enough to be seen.
          const r = halley.position.length();
          const seen = 1 - THREE.MathUtils.smoothstep(r, 0.8, 1.5);
          const active = 1 - THREE.MathUtils.smoothstep(r, 0.45, 1.15);
          comaMat.opacity = (0.35 + active * 0.5) * seen * dim;
          coma.scale.setScalar(0.035 + active * 0.055);

          if (active > 0.001) {
            sunward.copy(halley.position).normalize();
            ionTail.holder.quaternion.setFromUnitVectors(TAIL_AXIS, sunward);
            // The dust tail's lag: mostly anti-sunward, partly back along
            // the path just travelled. Real dust tails curve because the
            // grains stay on their own orbits after release rather than
            // being swept straight out; this is that, to one term.
            trailing.copy(prevHalley).sub(halley.position);
            if (trailing.lengthSq() > 1e-12) {
              trailing.normalize().multiplyScalar(0.9).add(sunward).normalize();
              dustTail.holder.quaternion.setFromUnitVectors(TAIL_AXIS, trailing);
            }
            const grow = active * active;
            ionTail.holder.scale.setScalar(0.45 + grow * 0.95);
            dustTail.holder.scale.setScalar(0.45 + grow * 0.85);
          }
          setTailStrength(ionTail, active * 0.3 * dim);
          setTailStrength(dustTail, active * 0.30 * dim);
        },
      };
      objects.projects = group;
    }

    // ---- Music: a pulsar, rotating beam + rhythmic flash ----
    {
      const group = new THREE.Group();
      placeObject(group, "music");
      anchor.add(group);

      // What's drawn here is the pulsar's *emission*, not its surface. A
      // neutron star is about 20km across - at anything like a truthful
      // size against the rest of this scene it is a sub-pixel dot, which is
      // exactly what it used to be: 10% of the frame was the glow sprite
      // and the star itself was 3%, so Music appeared to have no object at
      // all. The beams and magnetosphere genuinely do span an enormous
      // volume, so those carry the object and the core is left as the
      // pinprick it should be.
      const coreMat = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({
        color: 0xffffff,
        emissive: accentColor,
        emissiveIntensity: 1.6,
        roughness: 0.3,
      });
      const core = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.SphereGeometry(0.1, 16, 16), coreMat);
      accentTargets.push(coreMat.emissive);
      group.add(core);

      const coreGlow = makeGlowSprite(1.1, 0.85);
      const haloGlow = makeGlowSprite(2.6, 0.3);
      group.add(coreGlow, haloGlow);

      // The magnetosphere: a bright, tightly-wound equatorial disc. Gives
      // the beams something to be perpendicular to, so the object reads as
      // having an axis rather than being a dot with two spikes.
      const discMat = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({
        color: accentColor,
        vertexColors: true,
        transparent: true,
        opacity: 0.22,
        side: THREE.DoubleSide,
        depthWrite: false,
        ...ADDITIVE_WITH_ALPHA,
      });
      accentTargets.push(discMat.color);
      const DISC_INNER = 0.22;
      const DISC_OUTER = 1.25;
      const discGeo = new THREE.RingGeometry(DISC_INNER, DISC_OUTER, 96, 8);
      {
        // Same fade-to-black-instead-of-an-edge treatment as the beams: a
        // ring drawn at flat opacity ends in a hard circular cut, which is
        // the one thing a cloud of infalling plasma definitely does not do.
        const pos = discGeo.attributes.position;
        // RGBA, so alpha fades with the colour - see the note on the beam
        // ramp. Fading colour alone leaves the rim writing coverage with no
        // colour in it, which composites as a dark ring.
        const cols = new Float32Array(pos.count * 4);
        const v = new THREE.Vector3();
        for (let i = 0; i < pos.count; i++) {
          v.fromBufferAttribute(pos, i);
          // Clamped for the same reason as the beam ramp above - the outer
          // rim measures a shade past DISC_OUTER, which sent 1 - t negative
          // and turned the entire colour buffer into NaN.
          const t = THREE.MathUtils.clamp((v.length() - DISC_INNER) / (DISC_OUTER - DISC_INNER), 0, 1);
          const b = Math.pow(1 - t, 1.9);
          cols[i * 4] = b;
          cols[i * 4 + 1] = b;
          cols[i * 4 + 2] = b;
          cols[i * 4 + 3] = b;
        }
        discGeo.setAttribute("color", new THREE.BufferAttribute(cols, 4));
      }
      const disc = new THREE.Mesh(discGeo, discMat);
      disc.rotation.x = Math.PI / 2;
      group.add(disc);

      const beamGroup = new THREE.Group();
      // Tilted off the rotation axis, which is what makes a pulsar a
      // pulsar: the beams sweep a cone as the star turns, rather than
      // sitting still and spinning about their own length (invisible).
      beamGroup.rotation.z = 0.42;
      group.add(beamGroup);

      // A beam is light, and light doesn't have a silhouette or an end. A
      // plain additive cone has both: it reads as a solid grey megaphone,
      // and because it's open-ended and double-sided you see straight into
      // its far opening as a dark ellipse "cap". Fixed by baking a
      // brightness ramp into vertex colours - additive blending makes black
      // contribute exactly nothing, so a cone fading to black at its far
      // end simply stops existing there, no alpha or shader needed.
      const BEAM_LENGTH = 3.6;
      function makeBeamGeometry(radius: number, height: number): THREE.ConeGeometry {
        // Needs real height segments: the ramp is per-vertex, so a
        // single-segment cone could only fade between its two ends.
        const geo = new THREE.ConeGeometry(radius, height, 28, 16, true);
        const pos = geo.attributes.position;
        // Four components, not three. The blend accumulates alpha as well as
        // colour (see ADDITIVE_WITH_ALPHA), so alpha has to follow the same
        // ramp - otherwise the faded end of the beam writes full coverage
        // with no colour in it, which is premultiplied black, and the beam
        // ends in a dark smear over the page instead of in nothing.
        const colors = new Float32Array(pos.count * 4);
        for (let i = 0; i < pos.count; i++) {
          // 1 at the apex, which sits against the star; 0 at the open end.
          // Clamped before the pow: the generated vertices land a hair
          // outside [0, height] in float, and Math.pow() of a negative base
          // with a fractional exponent is NaN - which propagates into the
          // colour buffer and renders as garbage rather than as nothing.
          const t = THREE.MathUtils.clamp((pos.getY(i) + height / 2) / height, 0, 1);
          const v = Math.pow(t, 2.4);
          colors[i * 4] = v;
          colors[i * 4 + 1] = v;
          colors[i * 4 + 2] = v;
          colors[i * 4 + 3] = v;
        }
        geo.setAttribute("color", new THREE.BufferAttribute(colors, 4));
        return geo;
      }
      const beamMat = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({
        color: accentColor,
        vertexColors: true,
        transparent: true,
        opacity: 0.55,
        side: THREE.DoubleSide,
        depthWrite: false,
        ...ADDITIVE_WITH_ALPHA,
      });
      accentTargets.push(beamMat.color);
      const beamGeo = makeBeamGeometry(0.26, BEAM_LENGTH);
      [1, -1].forEach((dir) => {
        const beam = new THREE.Mesh(beamGeo, beamMat);
        // ConeGeometry puts its apex at +y. Rotating +90deg about z sends
        // +y to -x, which for the +x beam points the apex back at the star
        // and leaves the wide end outward - the way round emission actually
        // spreads. (The other sign gives two funnels pointing inward.)
        beam.rotation.z = dir > 0 ? Math.PI / 2 : -Math.PI / 2;
        beam.position.x = (dir * BEAM_LENGTH) / 2;
        beamGroup.add(beam);
      });

      // Tip the whole object out of the view plane so the magnetosphere
      // disc is seen as an ellipse rather than edge-on as a single line,
      // and so the beam sweep reads as a cone rather than a see-saw.
      group.rotation.set(0.5, 0, 0.16);

      group.userData = { coreGlow, haloGlow, coreMat, beamGroup, beamMat, discMat };
      objects.music = group;
    }

    // ---- Contact: a probe emitting expanding signal-ping rings ----
    {
      const group = new THREE.Group();
      placeObject(group, "contact");
      anchor.add(group);

      // The real Voyager, from NASA's own model (see
      // public/models/cosmos/License.txt). This was an octahedron, which
      // is what "weird geometrical shape emanating rings" describes
      // exactly - a probe is a specific, recognisable object and a
      // stand-in solid reads as a placeholder no matter how it's lit.
      // Unlike the Itokawa shape model this ships with baked textures, so
      // its materials are used as authored rather than overridden.
      //
      // Sits inside `body`, a pivot the animation tumbles, so the loaded
      // scene can be recentred on its own bounding box without the
      // recentring offset fighting the rotation.
      const body = new THREE.Group();
      group.add(body);
      // A neutral stand-in holds the object's place (and its measured
      // extent) until the 3MB model lands; swapped out on load, left alone
      // if the load fails.
      const placeholderMat = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({
        color: mutedColor,
        emissive: accentColor,
        emissiveIntensity: 0.35,
        roughness: 0.4,
        metalness: 0.3,
      });
      const placeholder = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.OctahedronGeometry(0.5, 0), placeholderMat);
      mutedTargets.push(placeholderMat.color);
      accentTargets.push(placeholderMat.emissive);
      body.add(placeholder);

      // Large relative to the ping rings below, which are what set this
      // object's measured extent and therefore its framing. At 1.5 the
      // rings filled the frame and Voyager sat in the middle of them at a
      // quarter of the height - concentric hoops with a speck inside, which
      // is the shape of the original complaint.
      const PROBE_SIZE = 2.9;
      gltfLoader.load(
        `${cosmosAssetBase}models/cosmos/voyager.glb`,
        (gltf: any) => {
          const probe = gltf.scene;
          const box = new THREE.Box3().setFromObject(probe);
          const span = box.getSize(new THREE.Vector3()).length();
          probe.scale.setScalar(PROBE_SIZE / (span || 1));
          // Recentre on the model's own bounds: NASA's export isn't
          // origin-centred, and an off-centre probe would swing around a
          // point outside itself once the tumble starts.
          const centre = box.getCenter(new THREE.Vector3()).multiplyScalar(PROBE_SIZE / (span || 1));
          probe.position.sub(centre);
          body.remove(placeholder);
          placeholder.geometry.dispose();
          placeholderMat.dispose();
          body.add(probe);
          // The probe is larger than the placeholder it replaced, so the
          // camera's parking distance has to be recomputed from it.
          refreshFraming("contact");
        },
        undefined,
        (err: unknown) => console.error(LOG, "Failed to load Voyager model", err),
      );

      // Signal pings: the reason this object is Contact rather than just a
      // spacecraft. Kept thin and faint so they read as a transmission
      // leaving the dish rather than as decorative hoops around it.
      const PING_COUNT = 3;
      const pingMat = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({
        color: accentColor,
        transparent: true,
        opacity: 0,
        side: THREE.DoubleSide,
        blending: THREE.AdditiveBlending,
        depthWrite: false,
      });
      const pings: THREE.Mesh[] = [];
      for (let i = 0; i < PING_COUNT; i++) {
        const ringMat = pingMat.clone();
        accentTargets.push(ringMat.color);
        const ring = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.RingGeometry(0.985, 1.0, 96), ringMat);
        // Tilted out of the view plane so they read as spherical wavefronts
        // leaving the spacecraft rather than as flat hoops drawn around it,
        // and offset per-ring so they don't stack into a bullseye.
        ring.rotation.x = 0.62;
        ring.rotation.z = i * 0.5;
        group.add(ring);
        pings.push(ring);
      }

      group.userData = { body, pings, pingPeriod: 3.4 };
      objects.contact = group;
    }

    // ---- Playground: a nebula ----
    {
      const group = new THREE.Group();
      placeObject(group, "playground");
      anchor.add(group);

      // The real Hubble photography is the nebula. Earlier this was the
      // other way round - two small photo puffs buried under a sprinkle of
      // 260 fat, fully-saturated point sprites - and the result read as
      // brightly coloured confetti orbiting an axis rather than as gas.
      // Now a dozen large, overlapping, individually-rotated crops of
      // "Pillars of Creation" and "Mystic Mountain" build the cloud, and
      // the point cloud is demoted to what it should always have been:
      // faint embedded stars.
      //
      // Each source photo is masked into a soft-edged texture exactly once
      // and shared by every sprite drawn from it (see makeMaskedTexture),
      // so twelve sprites cost two decodes, not twelve.
      const NEBULA_SOURCES = ["nebula-1.jpg", "nebula-2.jpg"];
      const nebulaSpriteMats: THREE.SpriteMaterial[] = [];

      // Radially masks a photo into a texture that fades to fully
      // transparent at its edge, so additively blended sprites overlap
      // without showing their square borders.
      function makeMaskedTexture(img: HTMLImageElement): THREE.CanvasTexture {
        const size = 512;
        const canvas = document.createElement("canvas");
        canvas.width = size;
        canvas.height = size;
        const ctx = canvas.getContext("2d")!;
        const srcSize = Math.min(img.width, img.height);
        ctx.drawImage(img, (img.width - srcSize) / 2, (img.height - srcSize) / 2, srcSize, srcSize, 0, 0, size, size);
        ctx.globalCompositeOperation = "destination-in";
        const gradient = ctx.createRadialGradient(size / 2, size / 2, 0, size / 2, size / 2, size / 2);
        gradient.addColorStop(0, "rgba(255,255,255,1)");
        gradient.addColorStop(0.45, "rgba(255,255,255,0.85)");
        gradient.addColorStop(1, "rgba(255,255,255,0)");
        ctx.fillStyle = gradient;
        ctx.fillRect(0, 0, size, size);
        const tex = new THREE.CanvasTexture(canvas);
        tex.colorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace;
        return tex;
      }

      // scale, x, y, z, material rotation (radians), opacity, source index.
      // Hand-placed rather than randomised: the two source photos each have
      // a strong directional structure, and letting them land anywhere
      // produced obvious repeats. Larger, fainter crops sit behind; smaller,
      // brighter ones pick out the core.
      // Six, not more. Each of these is a large additive quad and they
      // overlap by design, so the cloud's cost is essentially the number of
      // them times the screen area they cover - by some margin the most
      // expensive surface in the scene when you're inside it. Six still
      // reads as a continuous structure; the two that were cut were the
      // ones most redundant with their neighbours.
      const NEBULA_PUFFS: [number, number, number, number, number, number, number][] = [
        [3.6, -0.35,  0.10, -0.75, 0.0,  0.32, 0],
        [3.2,  0.55, -0.15, -0.45, 2.1,  0.28, 1],
        [2.7, -0.15, -0.45,  0.05, 4.0,  0.30, 0],
        [2.2,  0.55,  0.35,  0.25, 1.2,  0.26, 1],
        [1.9, -0.70,  0.28,  0.40, 3.3,  0.28, 1],
        [1.5,  0.05, -0.12,  0.62, 0.7,  0.32, 0],
      ];
      NEBULA_PUFFS.forEach(([scale, x, y, z, rotation, opacity, source]) => {
        const spriteMat = new THREE.SpriteMaterial({
          map: glowTexture,
          // Tinted until the photo lands, then handed over to the real
          // image - a slow or failed load degrades to the old flat glow
          // rather than to nothing.
          color: hueShift(accentColor, source ? -0.14 : 0.1, 1, 0.05),
          transparent: true,
          opacity,
          rotation,
          blending: THREE.AdditiveBlending,
          depthWrite: false,
        });
        const sprite = new THREE.Sprite(spriteMat);
        sprite.scale.set(scale, scale, 1);
        sprite.position.set(x, y, z);
        group.add(sprite);
        nebulaSpriteMats.push(spriteMat);
        spriteMat.userData = { source };
      });

      NEBULA_SOURCES.forEach((file, sourceIndex) => {
        textureLoader.load(
          `${cosmosAssetBase}textures/cosmos/${file}`,
          (loaded: THREE.Texture) => {
            const tex = makeMaskedTexture(loaded.image as HTMLImageElement);
            nebulaSpriteMats.forEach((m) => {
              if (m.userData.source !== sourceIndex) return;
              m.map = tex;
              m.color.set(0xffffff);
              m.needsUpdate = true;
            });
          },
          undefined,
          (err: unknown) => console.error(LOG, `Failed to load nebula photo ${file}`, err),
        );
      });

      // Embedded stars. Ten times the count at a fraction of the size, and
      // near-white rather than saturated accent hues - the job here is to
      // give the gas something to be lit from within, not to be the thing
      // you look at.
      const NEBULA_COUNT = 2200;
      const positions = new Float32Array(NEBULA_COUNT * 3);
      const colors = new Float32Array(NEBULA_COUNT * 3);
      for (let i = 0; i < NEBULA_COUNT; i++) {
        // Rough ellipsoid via three summed randoms (cheap approximation of a
        // center-weighted volume, no need for true gaussian sampling here).
        const rx = (Math.random() + Math.random() + Math.random() - 1.5) / 1.5;
        const ry = (Math.random() + Math.random() + Math.random() - 1.5) / 1.5;
        const rz = (Math.random() + Math.random() + Math.random() - 1.5) / 1.5;
        positions[i * 3] = rx * 1.9;
        positions[i * 3 + 1] = ry * 1.3;
        positions[i * 3 + 2] = rz * 1.4;
      }
      const geo = new THREE.BufferGeometry();
      geo.setAttribute("position", new THREE.BufferAttribute(positions, 3));
      const colorAttr = new THREE.BufferAttribute(colors, 3);
      geo.setAttribute("color", colorAttr);

      function paintNebulaColors() {
        const white = new THREE.Color(0xffffff);
        const huePalette = [
          white.clone().lerp(hueShift(accentColor, 0), 0.25),
          white.clone().lerp(hueShift(accentColor, 0.14, 1.1, 0.05), 0.35),
          white.clone().lerp(hueShift(accentColor, -0.16, 0.9, -0.02), 0.3),
          white,
        ];
        for (let i = 0; i < NEBULA_COUNT; i++) {
          const c = huePalette[i % huePalette.length];
          colorAttr.setXYZ(i, c.r, c.g, c.b);
        }
        colorAttr.needsUpdate = true;
      }
      paintNebulaColors();

      const mat = new THREE.PointsMaterial({
        // PointsMaterial.size is a world-space measurement that three.js
        // does NOT run through the object's scale matrix, unlike every
        // other transform here - so the group scale has to be applied by
        // hand or the cloud stays a sprinkle of pinpricks after the nebula
        // grows to 120 units across.
        size: 0.012 * OBJECT_LAYOUT.playground.scale,
        // Without a map a point renders as a hard square. That was
        // invisible while these were sub-pixel specks; at nebula scale
        // they're tens of units across and read as literal pixel blocks,
        // so they need the same soft radial falloff the glow sprites use.
        map: glowTexture,
        vertexColors: true,
        transparent: true,
        opacity: 0.9,
        blending: THREE.AdditiveBlending,
        depthWrite: false,
        sizeAttenuation: true,
      });
      const points = new THREE.Points(geo, mat);
      group.add(points);
      applyNebulaDrift(mat, motionClock, NEBULA_DRIFT);

      const repaintPuffs = () => {
        nebulaSpriteMats.forEach((m) => {
          if (m.map === glowTexture) m.color.copy(hueShift(accentColor, m.userData.source ? -0.14 : 0.1, 1, 0.05));
        });
      };

      group.userData = { points, paintNebulaColors, repaintPuffs };
      objects.playground = group;
    }

    // ---- Photography: a distant spiral galaxy ----
    {
      const group = new THREE.Group();
      placeObject(group, "photography");
      group.rotation.x = THREE.MathUtils.degToRad(58);
      anchor.add(group);

      // 420 points spread over two arms is roughly 200 per arm - close
      // enough to a dotted line that the spiral had to be inferred rather
      // than seen. Ten times as many, an order of magnitude smaller, is the
      // difference between a diagram of a galaxy and a galaxy.
      const GALAXY_COUNT = 4600;
      const ARMS = 2;
      // Fraction of the points that form the central bulge instead of the
      // arms. Real spirals are far brighter in the middle than any arm, and
      // without this the core was just wherever the arms happened to meet.
      // Kept low on purpose: a third of the points in the bulge swamped the
      // arms completely and the galaxy became a fuzzy ball.
      const BULGE_FRACTION = 0.13;
      const positions = new Float32Array(GALAXY_COUNT * 3);
      const colors = new Float32Array(GALAXY_COUNT * 3);
      const galaxyT = new Float32Array(GALAXY_COUNT);
      const bulgeCount = Math.floor(GALAXY_COUNT * BULGE_FRACTION);
      for (let i = 0; i < GALAXY_COUNT; i++) {
        if (i < bulgeCount) {
          // Centre-weighted blob: cube the uniform sample so density climbs
          // steeply toward the middle.
          const u = Math.random();
          const radius = u * u * u * 0.3;
          const theta = Math.random() * Math.PI * 2;
          const phi = Math.acos(2 * Math.random() - 1);
          galaxyT[i] = radius / 1.6;
          positions[i * 3] = radius * Math.sin(phi) * Math.cos(theta);
          // Flattened, not spherical - a bulge is a squashed ellipsoid.
          positions[i * 3 + 1] = radius * Math.cos(phi) * 0.42;
          positions[i * 3 + 2] = radius * Math.sin(phi) * Math.sin(theta);
          continue;
        }
        const t = (i - bulgeCount) / (GALAXY_COUNT - bulgeCount);
        galaxyT[i] = t;
        const armIndex = i % ARMS;
        const radius = Math.pow(t, 0.6) * 1.6;
        // Arm scatter grows with radius: arms are tightly defined near the
        // core and fray at the edge, and a constant jitter looked stencilled.
        const spread = 0.06 + t * 0.22;
        const angle = t * Math.PI * 5 + armIndex * ((Math.PI * 2) / ARMS) + (Math.random() - 0.5) * 0.5;
        positions[i * 3] = Math.cos(angle) * radius + (Math.random() - 0.5) * spread;
        positions[i * 3 + 1] = (Math.random() - 0.5) * 0.07 * (1 - t * 0.6);
        positions[i * 3 + 2] = Math.sin(angle) * radius + (Math.random() - 0.5) * spread;
      }
      const geo = new THREE.BufferGeometry();
      geo.setAttribute("position", new THREE.BufferAttribute(positions, 3));
      const colorAttr = new THREE.BufferAttribute(colors, 3);
      geo.setAttribute("color", colorAttr);

      // Per-star brightness, fixed once so repaints stay stable. Without
      // it every point is exactly as bright as every other and the arms
      // read as a stipple pattern - an even dot screen is the one thing no
      // photograph of a galaxy has ever looked like.
      const galaxyLum = new Float32Array(GALAXY_COUNT);
      for (let i = 0; i < GALAXY_COUNT; i++) {
        const r = Math.random();
        galaxyLum[i] = 0.3 + r * r * 0.7;
      }

      function paintGalaxyColors() {
        const core = new THREE.Color(0xfff2dc);
        const edge = hueShift(accentColor, 0.05, 1, 0.12);
        const mixed = new THREE.Color();
        for (let i = 0; i < GALAXY_COUNT; i++) {
          // Only the outer half of the arms takes the accent tint; holding
          // the inner arms near the core's warm white is what keeps the
          // spiral legible as a continuous structure rather than a bright
          // ball with a separate blue halo around it.
          mixed.copy(core).lerp(edge, Math.min(1, Math.max(0, galaxyT[i] - 0.15) * 1.4));
          mixed.multiplyScalar(galaxyLum[i]);
          colorAttr.setXYZ(i, mixed.r, mixed.g, mixed.b);
          if (i % HAZE_STRIDE === 0) {
            const h = i / HAZE_STRIDE;
            if (h < hazeCount) hazeColorAttr.setXYZ(h, mixed.r, mixed.g, mixed.b);
          }
        }
        colorAttr.needsUpdate = true;
        hazeColorAttr.needsUpdate = true;
      }

      const mat = new THREE.PointsMaterial({
        // Hand-applied group scale - see the note on the nebula's cloud.
        // 0.009 was too far the other way: individually correct-looking
        // stars that collectively left the arms below the threshold of
        // being visible at all from 660 units out.
        size: 0.022 * OBJECT_LAYOUT.photography.scale,
        map: glowTexture,
        vertexColors: true,
        transparent: true,
        opacity: 0.85,
        blending: THREE.AdditiveBlending,
        depthWrite: false,
        sizeAttenuation: true,
      });
      const points = new THREE.Points(geo, mat);
      group.add(points);

      // A second, much softer pass that turns a field of separate dots into
      // gas with stars in it. Built from every third point rather than all
      // of them: these sprites are ten times the area of the star points,
      // so they are the most expensive thing the galaxy draws, and at this
      // size and opacity a third of them overlap into the same continuous
      // haze the full set did.
      const HAZE_STRIDE = 2;
      const hazeCount = Math.floor(GALAXY_COUNT / HAZE_STRIDE);
      const hazePositions = new Float32Array(hazeCount * 3);
      const hazeColors = new Float32Array(hazeCount * 3);
      for (let i = 0; i < hazeCount; i++) {
        const src = i * HAZE_STRIDE;
        hazePositions[i * 3] = positions[src * 3];
        hazePositions[i * 3 + 1] = positions[src * 3 + 1];
        hazePositions[i * 3 + 2] = positions[src * 3 + 2];
      }
      const hazeGeo = new THREE.BufferGeometry();
      hazeGeo.setAttribute("position", new THREE.BufferAttribute(hazePositions, 3));
      const hazeColorAttr = new THREE.BufferAttribute(hazeColors, 3);
      hazeGeo.setAttribute("color", hazeColorAttr);
      // Left on the RGB-only blend on purpose, unlike the other additive
      // surfaces. This one genuinely does fade to black across its own
      // vertex colours, which is the case RGB-only exists for, and it is
      // drawn on top of the galaxy's own point cloud rather than over empty
      // space - so there is already alpha underneath it to composite against.
      const hazeMat = new THREE.PointsMaterial({
        size: 0.1 * OBJECT_LAYOUT.photography.scale,
        map: glowTexture,
        vertexColors: true,
        transparent: true,
        opacity: 0.12,
        depthWrite: false,
        sizeAttenuation: true,
        ...ADDITIVE_RGB_ONLY,
      });
      const haze = new THREE.Points(hazeGeo, hazeMat);
      group.add(haze);

      // Stars and haze are the same disc sampled twice, so they have to
      // shear as one thing or the galaxy separates into two galaxies.
      applyGalaxyShear(mat, motionClock, GALAXY_SHEAR);
      applyGalaxyShear(hazeMat, motionClock, GALAXY_SHEAR);

      // Painted only now: the ramp fills the star buffer and the haze
      // buffer in one pass, so it has to run after both exist.
      paintGalaxyColors();

      // Small and warm. A wide white glow here just washed the inner arms
      // out - the bulge's own point density is doing most of this work now.
      const coreGlow = makeGlowSprite(0.34, 0.42, new THREE.Color(0xfff0d4), null);
      group.add(coreGlow);

      group.userData = { points, haze, paintGalaxyColors, coreGlow };
      objects.photography = group;
    }
    } // end if (isDesktop)

    // Only used when there are no objects at all (mobile tier): a plain
    // wide shot of the starfield around the origin.
    const IDLE_CAMERA_Z = 13;

    // ---- Framing: parking distances derived from what was actually built ----
    // Each object's world-space extent, measured once from the finished
    // group. Everything that needs "how far back should the camera sit for
    // this object" goes through standoffFor(), so the answer can never
    // drift out of step with the geometry the way six hand-written numbers
    // did.

    // The order the journey visits the six objects in. Declared up here
    // rather than alongside the rest of the journey code below, because
    // waypointDepths() needs it and that runs during the first resize().
    const DEPTH_ORDER: CosmosKey[] = ["playground", "projects", "photography", "music", "about", "contact"];

    // Same sequence the homepage menu and the inner pages' approach scale
    // index against (lib/destinations.ts). It stays declared separately
    // because here it is also a depth ordering for the scene graph, typed
    // against CosmosKey - but a silent disagreement would mean the "02 / 06"
    // you clicked and the "02 / 06" you land under refer to different
    // objects, so it is checked rather than trusted. Dev-only: the whole
    // block drops out of the production bundle.
    if (import.meta.env.DEV && DEPTH_ORDER.join() !== DESTINATIONS.map((d) => d.slug).join()) {
      console.warn(
        "[cosmos] DEPTH_ORDER and lib/destinations.ts disagree:",
        DEPTH_ORDER.join(),
        "vs",
        DESTINATIONS.map((d) => d.slug).join(),
      );
    }

    const objectExtent: Partial<Record<CosmosKey, { x: number; y: number; z: number }>> = {};
    const measureBox = new THREE.Box3();
    const measureSize = new THREE.Vector3();
    const measureChildBox = new THREE.Box3();

    // Box3.setFromObject in a sentence, except that a subtree marked
    // userData.noExtent is skipped along with everything under it.
    //
    // That exists for one case: Halley's comet, whose aphelion is 1.7x
    // further out than Pluto's orbit. Framing is fitted to the measured
    // bounding box, so including it would shrink the entire solar system to
    // three-fifths of the frame in order to leave room for a comet that is
    // off past the edge of it for most of a lap - paying, permanently, for
    // something that isn't there. Measure the system; let the comet leave.
    function expandExtent(box: THREE.Box3, object: THREE.Object3D) {
      if (object.userData.noExtent) return;
      const geometry = (object as THREE.Mesh).geometry;
      if (geometry) {
        if (!geometry.boundingBox) geometry.computeBoundingBox();
        measureChildBox.copy(geometry.boundingBox!).applyMatrix4(object.matrixWorld);
        box.union(measureChildBox);
      }
      for (const child of object.children) expandExtent(box, child);
    }

    const measureOrigin = new THREE.Vector3();

    function measureExtent(key: CosmosKey) {
      const group = objects[key];
      if (!group) return;
      // Ancestors first (getWorldPosition walks up), then this subtree, so
      // every matrix expandExtent reads is current.
      group.getWorldPosition(measureOrigin);
      group.updateWorldMatrix(false, true);
      measureBox.makeEmpty();
      expandExtent(measureBox, group);
      // Not the box's size: twice its largest reach from the group's own
      // origin. The camera aims at the origin, so what has to fit on screen
      // is the furthest point from it, mirrored - and those two numbers only
      // agree for something centred on its own origin. Kepler's first law
      // guarantees the solar system isn't: the Sun sits at a *focus* of every
      // orbit, never the centre, so the system reaches further to one side
      // than the other and taking the size under-framed it enough to cut
      // Pluto off the edge.
      measureSize.set(
        Math.max(measureOrigin.x - measureBox.min.x, measureBox.max.x - measureOrigin.x),
        Math.max(measureOrigin.y - measureBox.min.y, measureBox.max.y - measureOrigin.y),
        Math.max(measureOrigin.z - measureBox.min.z, measureBox.max.z - measureOrigin.z),
      );
      objectExtent[key] = { x: measureSize.x * 2, y: measureSize.y * 2, z: measureSize.z * 2 };
    }

    // Re-measure and re-place after an asset that changes an object's size
    // arrives. The glTF models load long after this initial pass, so
    // without it the framing for Contact and Projects is derived from the
    // placeholder that was standing in - correct only by coincidence, and
    // silently wrong the moment either model's target size is touched.
    function refreshFraming(key: CosmosKey) {
      if (!framingReady) return;
      measureExtent(key);
      placeObjects();
    }

    (Object.keys(objects) as CosmosKey[]).forEach(measureExtent);
    framingReady = true;

    // Distance at which `frame` of the viewport is filled by the object,
    // in whichever axis is the tighter constraint. Recomputed rather than
    // cached because it depends on the live camera aspect - a galaxy that
    // fits a wide window edge-to-edge has to be backed away from in a
    // portrait one.
    function standoffFor(key: CosmosKey): number {
      const extent = objectExtent[key];
      const frame = OBJECT_LAYOUT[key].frame;
      if (!extent || frame <= 0) return IDLE_CAMERA_Z;
      const halfFovTan = Math.tan(THREE.MathUtils.degToRad(camera.fov) / 2);
      const forHeight = extent.y / (2 * frame * halfFovTan);
      const forWidth = extent.x / (2 * frame * halfFovTan * camera.aspect);
      // Never park inside the object itself, whatever the measurements say.
      //
      // The framing terms above only constrain how much of the frame the
      // object fills; nothing in them stops the camera ending up *within*
      // its bounding sphere, and for anything with real depth that means
      // the near plane starts slicing through it. Small compact objects
      // survive that - a planet is a point and never meets the near plane -
      // but a wide thin structure like the solar system's orbit rings gets
      // cut, and clipped geometry does not fade out politely: it ends in a
      // hard straight edge where the plane crossed it, so complete ellipses
      // render as arcs that stop in mid air with straight bars across them.
      // Standing off the full bounding radius makes that unrepresentable.
      const radius = Math.hypot(extent.x, extent.y, extent.z) / 2;
      return Math.max(forHeight, forWidth, camera.near * 2.5, radius * 1.15 + camera.near);
    }

    // How far apart consecutive waypoints sit. Two terms, because a leg has
    // two jobs and they scale with different objects:
    //
    //   LEG_CLEARANCE * standoff(from)  - room to get clear of the object
    //     being left, so the camera isn't still inside it when it turns.
    //   LEG_APPROACH  * standoff(to)    - the run-in, expressed in units of
    //     how far away the destination wants to be viewed from. This is the
    //     term that decides whether a leg has anything to look at: at the
    //     moment you clear the old object, the new one is LEG_APPROACH
    //     standoffs out, so it already fills roughly 1/LEG_APPROACH of the
    //     frame it will eventually fill.
    //
    // Fixed distances could not do this, and that was the bug: 1000 units is
    // a stroll between two galaxies and an interstellar void between two
    // spacecraft, and the old layout used exactly 1000 for both. Averaging
    // the two standoffs (the first attempt at this) was no better - one big
    // object at either end swamps the average and strands the small one.
    const LEG_CLEARANCE = 1.6;
    const LEG_APPROACH = 6;

    // Waypoint depths, accumulated along the journey order. The first sits
    // near the origin so the camera's initial position is somewhere sane.
    const FIRST_WAYPOINT_Z = -400;
    function waypointDepths(): Record<CosmosKey, number> {
      const out = {} as Record<CosmosKey, number>;
      let z = FIRST_WAYPOINT_Z;
      DEPTH_ORDER.forEach((key, i) => {
        if (i > 0) {
          const prev = DEPTH_ORDER[i - 1];
          z -= LEG_CLEARANCE * standoffFor(prev) + LEG_APPROACH * standoffFor(key);
        }
        out[key] = z;
      });
      return out;
    }

    // x/y offsets are multiples of the parking distance, so they have to
    // wait until that distance is known. Re-run on resize for the same
    // reason standoffFor() is: aspect changes move the goalposts.
    function placeObjects() {
      const depths = waypointDepths();
      (Object.keys(objects) as CosmosKey[]).forEach((key) => {
        const layout = OBJECT_LAYOUT[key];
        const standoff = standoffFor(key);
        objects[key]!.position.set(layout.offset[0] * standoff, layout.offset[1] * standoff, depths[key]);
      });
    }

    // ---- Defocus ----------------------------------------------------
    // The journey has one subject at a time and five other objects strung
    // out across 8000 units of depth around it. Dimming alone never sold
    // that: a sun 2000 units away is still a *sharp* sun, so it read as
    // something sitting in the same plane as the thing you'd stopped to
    // look at rather than as somewhere you hadn't arrived at yet - light
    // punching through the nebula instead of hanging behind it.
    //
    // Not a depth-buffer bokeh pass. A real one would need an aperture
    // wide enough to blur something 2000 units out while holding a
    // 3-unit-wide probe sharp, which is not a lens that exists.
    //
    // Only the objects actually being softened leave the main pass. Each
    // renders alone into a half-resolution target, gets blurred at a
    // quarter, and composites back over the frame. Everything sharp - the
    // stars, the dust, the waypoint you're parked on - stays in one
    // ordinary full-resolution multisampled pass, drawn exactly as it was
    // before any of this existed.
    //
    // The first version put *every* waypoint through a full-resolution
    // 4x-multisampled half-float target so that blur zero would composite
    // the sharp render untouched. It was continuous and it was correct and
    // it cost 45ms a frame at a dwell and 280ms mid-transit, against 4.7ms
    // for the whole scene before it. A 3200x1900 multisampled half-float
    // buffer is around 190MB; clearing and resolving one per visible
    // object per frame is not a thing a page can do. Blurred objects are
    // dim, distant and about to be unrecognisable, so half resolution and
    // no multisampling costs nothing that can be seen - and the sharp
    // object, which is the one you'd notice, never enters the pipeline.
    //
    // Ordering assumes every softened object sits behind the sharp one.
    // That holds by construction: at waypoint i the camera parks at
    // depth[i] + standoff, so waypoints before i are behind it and only
    // the ones ahead are still on screen.
    const DEFOCUS_LAYER_SCALE = 0.5;
    const DEFOCUS_BLUR_SCALE = 0.25;
    // Gaussian sigma in CSS pixels at full defocus. Chosen by eye against
    // the sun at Playground: enough that it reads as a glow you can't
    // resolve, not so much that it becomes a flat wash.
    const DEFOCUS_MAX_PX = 12;
    // Below this an object is still to all appearances sharp, so it stays
    // in the main pass and pays nothing. Low enough that the half-
    // resolution layer it crosses into is already carrying enough blur to
    // hide the resolution it lost on the way in.
    const DEFOCUS_MIN = 0.05;
    const DEFOCUS_MAX_LAYERS = 2;
    let rtLayer: THREE.WebGLRenderTarget | null = null;
    let rtPing: THREE.WebGLRenderTarget | null = null;
    let rtPong: THREE.WebGLRenderTarget | null = null;
    const drawSize = new THREE.Vector2();

    const quadCamera = new THREE.OrthographicCamera(-1, 1, 1, -1, 0, 1);
    const quadScene = new THREE.Scene();
    const QUAD_VERT = `
      varying vec2 vUv;
      void main() {
        vUv = uv;
        gl_Position = vec4(position.xy, 0.0, 1.0);
      }
    `;
    // The standard nine-tap gaussian folded into five linear-sampled taps.
    // Run separably and twice over, with the second pair at a wider spacing
    // than the first: two moderate gaussians compose into one wide one, and
    // the mismatched spacings keep the five taps from leaving a visible
    // comb at the widest settings.
    const blurMaterial = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({
      uniforms: { tDiffuse: { value: null }, uStep: { value: new THREE.Vector2() } },
      vertexShader: QUAD_VERT,
      fragmentShader: `
        uniform sampler2D tDiffuse;
        uniform vec2 uStep;
        varying vec2 vUv;
        void main() {
          vec4 c = texture2D(tDiffuse, vUv) * 0.2270270270;
          c += texture2D(tDiffuse, vUv + uStep * 1.3846153846) * 0.3162162162;
          c += texture2D(tDiffuse, vUv - uStep * 1.3846153846) * 0.3162162162;
          c += texture2D(tDiffuse, vUv + uStep * 3.2307692308) * 0.0702702703;
          c += texture2D(tDiffuse, vUv - uStep * 3.2307692308) * 0.0702702703;
          gl_FragColor = c;
        }
      `,
      depthTest: false,
      depthWrite: false,
    });
    // Everything in the offscreen targets is linear and premultiplied: the
    // renderer only applies tone mapping and the output transform when it
    // is drawing to the canvas, so this pass owes both.
    //
    // The unpremultiply/re-premultiply around them is not ceremony. sRGB
    // encoding raises 0.5 to 0.73, so encoding a premultiplied colour in
    // place can leave RGB above alpha - the exact illegal state that made
    // the orbits vanish on a real compositor. Encoding the straight colour
    // and multiplying alpha back in afterwards cannot.
    //
    // Alpha is taken as the larger of the layer's own alpha and its
    // brightest channel, which costs nothing where the two already agree
    // and rescues anything additive that wrote colour without coverage
    // (the galaxy haze still does, deliberately) instead of dividing by a
    // near-zero alpha and blowing out.
    //
    // Added, not composited over. "Over" darkens the destination by the
    // layer's alpha everywhere the layer has any at all - and a blurred
    // corona has a little alpha across its entire rectangle. The result is
    // a faint square veil over whatever is behind it, with a hard edge
    // exactly where the scissor stops. No pad is large enough to fix that:
    // a soft glow has no edge to contain, so wherever the rectangle ends,
    // it shows.
    //
    // Adding also happens to be exactly what these objects did before any
    // of this existed. Encoding the straight colour and multiplying alpha
    // back in reproduces the hardware's own SrcAlpha/One arithmetic term
    // for term, so a layer now composites to the same pixels the direct
    // additive pass produced. Alpha accumulates too - see
    // ADDITIVE_WITH_ALPHA above for what happens on a real compositor when
    // it doesn't.
    const compositeMaterial = new THREE.ShaderMaterial({
      uniforms: { tBlur: { value: null } },
      vertexShader: QUAD_VERT,
      fragmentShader: `
        uniform sampler2D tBlur;
        varying vec2 vUv;
        void main() {
          vec4 texel = texture2D(tBlur, vUv);
          float a = clamp(max(texel.a, max(texel.r, max(texel.g, texel.b))), 0.0, 1.0);
          if (a <= 0.0001) discard;
          gl_FragColor = vec4(texel.rgb / a, a);
          #include <tonemapping_fragment>
          #include <colorspace_fragment>
          gl_FragColor.rgb *= gl_FragColor.a;
        }
      `,
      transparent: true,
      depthTest: false,
      depthWrite: false,
      blending: THREE.CustomBlending,
      blendEquation: THREE.AddEquation,
      blendSrc: THREE.OneFactor,
      blendDst: THREE.OneFactor,
      blendEquationAlpha: THREE.AddEquation,
      blendSrcAlpha: THREE.OneFactor,
      blendDstAlpha: THREE.OneFactor,
    });
    const quad = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.PlaneGeometry(2, 2), blurMaterial);
    quad.frustumCulled = false;
    quadScene.add(quad);

    function sizeDefocusTargets() {
      renderer.getDrawingBufferSize(drawSize);
      const lw = Math.max(1, Math.floor(drawSize.x * DEFOCUS_LAYER_SCALE));
      const lh = Math.max(1, Math.floor(drawSize.y * DEFOCUS_LAYER_SCALE));
      const bw = Math.max(1, Math.floor(drawSize.x * DEFOCUS_BLUR_SCALE));
      const bh = Math.max(1, Math.floor(drawSize.y * DEFOCUS_BLUR_SCALE));
      if (!rtLayer) {
        // Half float, not the default eight bits per channel. What lands in
        // here is linear, un-tone-mapped scene light, and the composite
        // sRGB-encodes it afterwards - which stretches the darkest eighth
        // of the range over a third of the output. Eight bits of linear
        // does not survive that: the pulsar's halo came back as a stack of
        // concentric rings. Falls back to eight-bit where a float colour
        // buffer isn't renderable, since banded is still better than blank.
        //
        // No multisampling. These three are only ever asked to hold
        // something that is about to be blurred past the point where a
        // jagged edge exists.
        const type = renderer.getContext().getExtension("EXT_color_buffer_float")
          ? THREE.HalfFloatType
          : THREE.UnsignedByteType;
        rtLayer = new THREE.WebGLRenderTarget(lw, lh, { type });
        rtPing = new THREE.WebGLRenderTarget(bw, bh, { depthBuffer: false, type });
        rtPong = new THREE.WebGLRenderTarget(bw, bh, { depthBuffer: false, type });
        return;
      }
      rtLayer.setSize(lw, lh);
      rtPing!.setSize(bw, bh);
      rtPong!.setSize(bw, bh);
    }

    // ---- Adaptive glare shield ----
    //
    // Where the reading column ends, as a percentage of the viewport, so the
    // shield below can be masked to it. Measured from the page's real
    // <section> rather than derived from --max-width: that token is in ch,
    // which resolves against whatever font actually loaded, and the padding
    // around it differs per route.
    const glareEl = document.getElementById("cosmos-glare");

    // Scales the shield down where the mask cannot release it (see below).
    let glareWidthFactor = 1;

    function measureColumn() {
      if (!glareEl) return;
      const section = document.querySelector("#main section") as HTMLElement | null;
      const w = window.innerWidth;
      // Below ~1100px framingOffsetX recentres the object behind the column
      // (see backdropStandoffMultiplier), so there is no free side to
      // preserve and the mask covers everything.
      //
      // Which means every pixel of the object gets the full shield, with no
      // bright side left anywhere to say what it is - a star came out as a
      // flat grey-brown disc. Wide viewports can afford a strong shield
      // precisely because the mask hands most of the object back; without
      // that, the same strength is simply the old flat wash again, and much
      // heavier. The text has room to spare here anyway: the object hangs
      // 1.3x further back at these widths, so it is smaller and dimmer to
      // begin with.
      if (!section || w < 1100) {
        glareWidthFactor = 1;
        container!.style.setProperty("--cosmos-column-edge", "100%");
        container!.style.setProperty("--cosmos-column-fade", "100%");
        return;
      }
      glareWidthFactor = 1;
      const right = (section.getBoundingClientRect().right / w) * 100;
      container!.style.setProperty("--cosmos-column-edge", `${Math.min(96, right + 3).toFixed(1)}%`);
      container!.style.setProperty("--cosmos-column-fade", `${Math.min(100, right + 22).toFixed(1)}%`);
    }

    // How much light each object actually throws at the page. A sun behind a
    // paragraph and a grey spacecraft behind the same paragraph are not the
    // same problem, and shading them equally would mean either under-serving
    // the sun or pointlessly dimming the probe.
    const GLARE_WEIGHT: Record<CosmosKey, number> = {
      about: 0.78,     // a photospheric disc; the worst case by a distance
      photography: 0.8,  // bright core, and large enough to fill the frame
      playground: 0.7,   // luminous but diffuse
      projects: 0.85,    // thin near-white rings, and they cross everything
      music: 0.72,      // small core, but the beams are bright and sweep
      contact: 0.55,     // dim overall; the manifest carries its own wash
    };

    const glareVec = new THREE.Vector3();
    // Live values, and the last ones actually written to the element - an
    // unchanged frame writes nothing at all. In backdrop mode the camera is
    // parked and only sways with the pointer, so this skips most frames.
    let glareX = 0;
    let glareY = 0;
    let glareScale = 1;
    let glareOpacity = 0;
    let glareWritten = false;
    let glareLastX = 0;
    let glareLastY = 0;
    let glareLastScale = 1;
    let glareLastOpacity = 0;

    function updateGlare(dt: number) {
      if (!glareEl) return;
      // On a route with no waypoint of its own - 404, or anything else off
      // the six-object map - focusKey is null, and computeCameraTarget falls
      // back to the first waypoint rather than pointing at nothing. The
      // shield has to make the same choice or it tracks a different object
      // than the camera is framing: on /404/ that meant no shield at all,
      // with the nebula at full brightness directly behind the copy.
      const key =
        cosmosMode !== "backdrop" ? null
        : focusKey ?? (isDesktop ? DEPTH_ORDER[0] : null);
      let targetOpacity = 0;

      if (key && objects[key]) {
        objects[key]!.getWorldPosition(glareVec);
        const distance = camera.position.distanceTo(glareVec);
        const extent = objectExtent[key];
        glareVec.project(camera);
        // z > 1 means behind the camera or past the far plane, where the
        // projected x/y are meaningless (and mirrored).
        if (distance > 0 && extent && glareVec.z <= 1) {
          const w = window.innerWidth;
          const h = window.innerHeight;
          glareX = (glareVec.x * 0.5 + 0.5) * w;
          glareY = (-glareVec.y * 0.5 + 0.5) * h;
          // The wider of the two axes, because what needs shading is the
          // object's whole bright footprint - the solar system's rings
          // spread far wider than they are tall, and it is the rings that
          // cut through the text.
          const halfExtent = Math.max(extent.x, extent.y) / 2;
          const halfFovTan = Math.tan(THREE.MathUtils.degToRad(camera.fov) / 2);
          const radius = (halfExtent * h) / (2 * distance * halfFovTan);
          // 1.35 so the shade reaches a little past the object itself: a
          // hard-edged pool exactly the size of the sun would put its own
          // boundary through the middle of a line of text.
          //
          // The floor matters more than it looks. A radial pool centred on
          // the object assumes the object's light is concentrated there,
          // which is true of a star and false of the two extended ones: the
          // solar system is thin bright rings spanning the whole frame, and
          // the galaxy has a halo well outside its core. For those, the
          // brightness a reader meets is far from the centre the shield is
          // parked on, and a tightly-sized pool leaves it uncovered - which
          // is exactly where the filter bar measured 1.1:1 against the
          // rings. Never smaller than most of the viewport, so the falloff
          // is still meaningful by the time it reaches the column.
          const minRadius = h * 0.75;
          // The star's ejection reaches past the extent this is sized from,
          // so while one is erupting the shield grows with it (see
          // flareGlare). Nothing else in the scene leaves its own bounds.
          const transient = key === "about" ? 1 + flareGlare * 0.55 : 1;
          glareScale = (Math.max(minRadius, radius * 1.35) * transient) / 500;
          targetOpacity = GLARE_WEIGHT[key] * glareWidthFactor;
        }
      }

      // Eased, so arriving on a route fades the shade in with the camera
      // rather than stamping it on at full strength the moment focusKey is
      // assigned. Position and size are *not* eased - they follow the object
      // exactly, because a shadow that lags the thing casting it is a shadow
      // you can see moving.
      glareOpacity += (targetOpacity - glareOpacity) * easeFactor(0.08, dt);

      if (
        !glareWritten ||
        Math.abs(glareX - glareLastX) > 1 ||
        Math.abs(glareY - glareLastY) > 1 ||
        Math.abs(glareScale - glareLastScale) > 0.004 ||
        Math.abs(glareOpacity - glareLastOpacity) > 0.004
      ) {
        glareEl.style.transform =
          `translate3d(${glareX.toFixed(1)}px, ${glareY.toFixed(1)}px, 0) scale(${glareScale.toFixed(3)})`;
        glareEl.style.opacity = glareOpacity.toFixed(3);
        glareWritten = true;
        glareLastX = glareX;
        glareLastY = glareY;
        glareLastScale = glareScale;
        glareLastOpacity = glareOpacity;
      }
    }

    function resize() {
      const rect = container!.getBoundingClientRect();
      const w = rect.width || 1;
      const h = rect.height || 1;
      renderer.setSize(w, h, false);
      camera.aspect = w / h;
      camera.updateProjectionMatrix();
      if (rtLayer) sizeDefocusTargets();
      placeObjects();
      measureColumn();
    }
    resize();
    window.addEventListener("resize", resize);

    starfield.setAccent(accentColor);

    let tiltX = 0;
    let tiltY = 0;
    let targetTiltX = 0;
    let targetTiltY = 0;
    // Drag-to-look, held apart from the ambient tilt above: that one tracks
    // the pointer's absolute position and is always live, this one
    // accumulates while a drag is held and springs back to nothing after.
    // They add.
    let dragging = false;
    let dragPointer = -1;
    let dragLastX = 0;
    let dragLastY = 0;
    let dragTiltX = 0;
    let dragTiltY = 0;
    let clock = 0;
    // rAF's own timestamp, so the first frame after a tab regains focus is
    // measured from the frame that actually preceded it.
    let prevFrameTime = 0;
    let prevCameraZ = camera.position.z;

    // ---- Hover/focus choreography: HomeMenu dispatches cosmos:focus /
    // cosmos:blur on document as the user hovers/keyboard-focuses a nav
    // item. Rather than hand-tuned per-object camera presets, we aim the
    // camera at whichever object's *live* world position (it keeps moving
    // due to auto-rotation) and dolly in slightly, dimming the rest. ----
    const ALL_KEYS = Object.keys(objects) as CosmosKey[];
    let focusKey: CosmosKey | null = null;
    const dimCurrent: Record<CosmosKey, number> = { about: 1, projects: 1, playground: 1, music: 1, photography: 1, contact: 1 };
    // 0 = in focus, 1 = as out of focus as the scene goes. Eased on the
    // same lag as dimCurrent so the two always move together - they are two
    // halves of one effect, and a waypoint that dimmed before it softened
    // (or after) reads as two separate things happening.
    const blurCurrent: Record<CosmosKey, number> = { about: 0, projects: 0, playground: 0, music: 0, photography: 0, contact: 0 };
    // Hover-to-flare: pointing at a label in the menu brightens and sharpens
    // the object that label leads to, before you commit to going there.
    //
    // Deliberately separate from focusKey, which the fallback menu already
    // drives. focusKey *replaces* the dim/blur targets, which is right for
    // an inner page (nothing else is happening) and wrong on the homepage,
    // where the scroll journey owns those targets and a hover would be
    // fighting it. This rides on top of whatever the journey has decided
    // instead, so the two compose.
    let hoverKey: CosmosKey | null = null;
    const hoverCurrent: Record<CosmosKey, number> = { about: 0, projects: 0, playground: 0, music: 0, photography: 0, contact: 0 };
    // Above 1: the point is to overdrive an object that is already at full
    // brightness, since the one being hovered is often the focused one.
    const HOVER_GAIN = 0.55;
    const HOVER_LEAN = 0.06;
    // The eased state behind dimCurrent/blurCurrent. Those two are what the
    // rest of the scene reads; these are what the lerp accumulates on, kept
    // apart so the hover lift can be applied on top without compounding.
    const dimBase: Record<CosmosKey, number> = { about: 1, projects: 1, playground: 1, music: 1, photography: 1, contact: 1 };
    const blurBase: Record<CosmosKey, number> = { about: 0, projects: 0, playground: 0, music: 0, photography: 0, contact: 0 };
    // Declared up here with the state they belong to rather than beside
    // renderFrame() below: applyRouteFraming() renders a frame of its own
    // and can run from a page-load event that lands while this module is
    // still evaluating, which would hit the dead zone of a `const` further
    // down. The same race that silently swallowed two of the models.
    const defocusLayers: { key: CosmosKey; distance: number }[] = [];
    const defocusWasVisible: boolean[] = [];
    const objectWorldPos = new THREE.Vector3();
    const framedTarget = new THREE.Vector3();
    // Written by computeCameraTarget alongside framedTarget, for the same
    // reason that function exists at all: the animation loop and the
    // instant (reduced-motion) path both hand the result to the rig, and a
    // second place deciding the orbit angle is a second place for the two
    // to disagree about what shot the page is in.
    let framedYaw = 0;
    let framedPitch = 0;
    const cullWorldPos = new THREE.Vector3();
    // Roughly 0.6% of the frame height at this field of view. Below that an
    // object is a couple of pixels; the depth haze has it near the dim floor
    // anyway, so nothing visible is lost by not drawing it.
    const CULL_ANGULAR_SIZE = 0.006;
    const cameraRig = createCameraRig(camera, 0.06);

    // ---- Homepage spatial journey: scrolling the tall container HomeMenu
    // renders drives a continuous depth value across the same six waypoints
    // hover-focus above uses, in the menu's own narrative order, instead of
    // one-object-at-a-time hover framing. Confined to the desktop hero
    // (homepage) state: inner "backdrop" pages have no tall scroll
    // container and keep using hover/route framing above unchanged. ----
    // Fraction of each leg's scroll spent held on the waypoint you're
    // leaving and the one you're reaching. Without these the mapping from
    // scroll to depth was linear, which meant the camera was only ever
    // *exactly* at an object at one infinitesimal scroll position and spent
    // the rest of the time in the empty space between two - so a section
    // never held focus for long enough to look at, which is precisely how
    // it felt. Now most of each leg is a dwell and the move happens in the
    // middle, with a smootherstep giving the accelerate/travel/decelerate/
    // settle shape rather than a constant-velocity slide.
    const LEG_HOLD_START = 0.42;
    const LEG_HOLD_END = 0.80;
    // Deliberately tight. The rig's lerp is exponential, so a fixed rate
    // leaves a residual error proportional to the distance travelled - and
    // a leg here can be 2000 units long while the pulsar wants to be viewed
    // from 14. Being "99% of the way there" frames the galaxy perfectly and
    // leaves the pulsar a tenth of its intended size, which is most of why
    // some waypoints looked like they never arrived. The cinematic shape
    // comes from the smootherstep on the *target* (above), not from this;
    // this only needs to be smooth enough not to read as a snap.
    const JOURNEY_EASE = 0.22;
    // Objects away from the current waypoint fade rather than vanish - this
    // is the scene's depth haze. Deliberately gentle: distance already
    // shrinks them to specks, and the far ones (a galaxy hanging behind the
    // nebula) are the whole reason the vista reads as deep, so dimming them
    // out of existence would throw away the effect the layout just bought.
    // Was 0.3/0.35, which left the *next* waypoint - one leg away, so a
    // distance of exactly 1 - sitting at 65% brightness. That is most of
    // full strength, and it is why the sun read as shining through the
    // nebula rather than waiting a journey behind it. At 0.62 a neighbour
    // lands at 38% and is on the floor by a leg and a half. The floor
    // itself is deliberately not zero: the deep vista is the point, and an
    // object you can just make out ahead is what tells you there is
    // somewhere to go.
    const DIM_FLOOR = 0.22;
    const DIM_FALLOFF = 0.62;
    // Defocus ramp, in the same units: journey distance in legs. Held at
    // zero for the first sixth of a leg so that a waypoint stays properly
    // sharp through its dwell and the first moments of leaving it, rather
    // than going soft the instant the scroll ticks.
    const DEFOCUS_START = 0.16;
    const DEFOCUS_FULL = 0.9;
    const journeyPointA = new THREE.Vector3();
    const journeyPointB = new THREE.Vector3();
    function journeyActive(): boolean {
      return isDesktop && cosmosMode === "hero" && legLength() > 0;
    }
    // Length of one leg in pixels, taken from the scroll track HomeMenu
    // actually rendered rather than assumed to be one viewport - the track
    // height is set in that component's CSS, and reading it here keeps the
    // two from silently disagreeing when it changes.
    //
    // Returns 0 to mean "there is no journey": the track is absent, or has
    // been hidden by HomeMenu's media query. That happens for real - this
    // component decides once per tab whether to build the six-object scene,
    // so a visitor who narrows the window past 900px keeps the scene while
    // the track disappears from under it. Previously offsetHeight went to 0,
    // the leg collapsed to a single pixel, and any scroll position at all
    // divided out to the last waypoint - the camera snapped to Contact and
    // stayed there.
    function legLength(): number {
      const track = document.getElementById("home-journey");
      if (!track) return 0;
      const vh = window.innerHeight || 1;
      const scrollable = track.offsetHeight - vh;
      if (scrollable < vh * 0.5) return 0;
      return scrollable / (DEPTH_ORDER.length - 1);
    }

    function journeyDepthProgress(): number {
      const leg = legLength();
      if (leg <= 0) return 0;
      const max = DEPTH_ORDER.length - 1;
      const raw = THREE.MathUtils.clamp(window.scrollY / leg, 0, max);
      const i = Math.floor(raw);
      const f = raw - i;
      const t = THREE.MathUtils.clamp((f - LEG_HOLD_START) / (LEG_HOLD_END - LEG_HOLD_START), 0, 1);
      // smootherstep - zero velocity *and* zero acceleration at both ends,
      // so arriving at a waypoint settles rather than stopping dead.
      return i + t * t * t * (t * (t * 6 - 15) + 10);
    }
    // Inner pages settle a little further out than the homepage's own
    // waypoint shot, but only a little: you flew here, and the object you
    // flew to should still be in the room with you rather than retreating
    // into wallpaper. (This was 1.8 - far enough that a probe or a pulsar
    // shrank back to a speck the moment you arrived, which undercut the
    // whole journey.) Expressed as a multiple of the object's own standoff
    // rather than a fixed z, since "a bit further back" means something
    // different for a probe than for a galaxy.
    // Under 1 on a wide viewport: an inner page frames its object *closer*
    // than the homepage waypoint shot did, not further out. The scene stops
    // being something beside the page and becomes something the page is
    // inside - the object crosses behind the text column rather than
    // sitting in the margin next to it, which is the whole point of keeping
    // it live back there instead of shipping a still image.
    //
    // Below ~1100px there is no margin to offset into, so framingOffsetX
    // recentres the object and it lands squarely behind the paragraph
    // rather than across one edge of it. Bringing it closer there buys
    // nothing and costs legibility on every line, so it goes the other way
    // and hangs back as a distant backdrop instead.
    function backdropStandoffMultiplier(): number {
      return window.innerWidth >= 1100 ? 0.86 : 1.3;
    }

    // ---- The approach ----
    //
    // An inner page is the last leg of the homepage's journey rather than a
    // document with a picture behind it, so its scroll drives the camera
    // too: reading down the page closes on the object the page is named
    // after and swings a little way around it. lib/approach.ts owns the
    // progress value (see the ownership note there); this only consumes it.
    //
    // t = 0 is deliberately the exact pose that shipped before any of this
    // existed - the multiplier is 1, the orbit angles are 0 - which means a
    // page too short to scroll, a visitor who never scrolls, and reduced
    // motion all land on the shot that was already framed and contrast-
    // verified. The approach can only ever move away from a known-good
    // starting point, never into one.
    function approachProgress(): number {
      return window.__approach?.t ?? 0;
    }

    // How much of the standoff is given up by the end of the leg. 0.62 is
    // a real closing - the object roughly half again as large in frame -
    // without crossing into the range where it fills the column behind the
    // text and the glare shield has to do all the work.
    const APPROACH_CLOSE = 0.62;
    // Radians of orbit at t = 1: ~17 degrees of yaw and ~5 of rise. Kept
    // small on purpose. This is the difference between a zoom and a pass -
    // the probe shows a different face of itself, the galaxy's disc opens
    // slightly - but past about 25 degrees the framing offset starts having
    // to fight the perspective to keep the object off the text.
    const APPROACH_YAW = 0.3;
    const APPROACH_PITCH = 0.09;
    // Star-field swing over the leg, in the same units the mouse tilt uses
    // (which peaks around 0.04), so this is an order of magnitude larger -
    // deliberately. Mouse tilt is meant to be felt and not noticed; this is
    // meant to be the thing that tells a phone visitor, who has no scene,
    // that they are moving.
    const APPROACH_SWING = 0.5;

    // Below 1100px framingOffsetX recentres the object behind the column
    // and backdropStandoffMultiplier pushes it back to compensate (see
    // both). Closing in there would walk a bright object straight into the
    // paragraph the visitor is reading, so the leg is flown at a fraction
    // of its width - present, but not at the cost of the words.
    function approachScale(): number {
      return window.innerWidth >= 1100 ? 1 : 0.35;
    }
    let cosmosMode: "hero" | "backdrop" = "hero";

    // ---- Warp dive (see the cosmos:warp handler below for the full
    // rationale). Held here so computeCameraTarget can give it priority
    // over every other framing source. ----
    let warpKey: CosmosKey | null = null;
    const WARP_DIVE_FACTOR = 0.34;
    // Never dive closer than this. The camera's near plane sits at 0.5 and
    // the smallest objects already park barely a unit out, so an unclamped
    // fraction would push the destination *behind* the near plane and clip
    // it out of existence at the exact moment it should be filling the
    // frame.
    const WARP_DIVE_MIN = 0.62;
    function warpStandoff(key: CosmosKey): number {
      return Math.max(standoffFor(key) * WARP_DIVE_FACTOR, WARP_DIVE_MIN);
    }

    // How far off-centre to frame the focused object. Returned in world
    // units at the given distance, which keeps the on-screen placement
    // constant even as the standoff swings between 1 and 1050 units across
    // the journey.
    //
    // The homepage nudges it right of centre to clear the readout column on
    // the left; inner pages push it further still, toward the right of the
    // centered 70ch content column - which is the same band the backdrop
    // scrim deliberately leaves thin (see .cosmos-scrim).
    //
    // Was 0.84/0.78, which parked the object out in the right margin
    // entirely, clear of the text. That kept it perfectly legible and made
    // it decoration. Bringing it back over the column's right edge means
    // the text is written across the object rather than next to it, and
    // the scrim and the text's own halo now carry legibility instead of
    // distance doing it.
    //
    // Below ~1100px there is no margin at all, so it recentres and lets the
    // flat scrim carry legibility on its own.
    function framingOffsetX(distance: number): number {
      const visibleHeight = 2 * distance * Math.tan(THREE.MathUtils.degToRad(camera.fov) / 2);
      const visibleWidth = visibleHeight * camera.aspect;
      const wide = window.innerWidth >= 1200;
      let screenFraction: number;
      if (cosmosMode === "backdrop") {
        screenFraction = window.innerWidth >= 1100 ? (wide ? 0.76 : 0.71) : 0.5;
      } else {
        screenFraction = wide ? 0.6 : 0.55;
      }
      return (screenFraction - 0.5) * visibleWidth;
    }

    // Writes the framed look-at point into `out` and returns the standoff
    // the camera should hold. Single source of truth for camera aim, shared
    // by the animation loop and the instant (reduced-motion) path so the
    // two can't drift apart.
    function computeCameraTarget(out: THREE.Vector3): number {
      let standoff: number;
      // Straight-on unless the backdrop branch below says otherwise. Set
      // here rather than in each of the other branches so that a shot which
      // never mentions orbit cannot inherit the previous frame's angle -
      // navigating from the bottom of a page into a warp dive did exactly
      // that, and the dive arrived crabbing.
      framedYaw = 0;
      framedPitch = 0;
      if (warpKey && objects[warpKey]) {
        // Mid-navigation: overrides both the scroll journey and hover
        // focus, so the dive can't be fought by whatever the page happens
        // to be doing while it plays out.
        objects[warpKey]!.getWorldPosition(objectWorldPos);
        standoff = warpStandoff(warpKey);
      } else if (journeyActive()) {
        // ---- Travelling between two waypoints ----
        //
        // Distance is interpolated *geometrically*, not linearly, and that
        // is the difference between a leg you can follow and a leg that is
        // simply empty. Adjacent objects here differ in scale by up to 47x
        // (a galaxy viewed from 657 units, then a pulsar viewed from 14).
        // Moving the camera at a constant rate through that means almost
        // the whole leg is spent too far from the destination to see it and
        // too far past the origin for that to still be there - which is
        // exactly the "I have no idea where I am" stretch. No choice of gap
        // length fixes it, because the problem is the *rate*: linear motion
        // between two wildly different scales spends its time in the middle,
        // where by definition nothing is close.
        //
        // Halving the remaining distance in equal steps instead - constant
        // proportional approach, the way distance actually behaves when you
        // travel toward something - keeps the destination growing steadily
        // in frame the whole way. It is also just how approach feels: the
        // last kilometre of a long trip is not the same as the first.
        const progress = journeyDepthProgress();
        const i0 = Math.floor(progress);
        const i1 = Math.min(i0 + 1, DEPTH_ORDER.length - 1);
        const frac = progress - i0;
        objects[DEPTH_ORDER[i0]]!.getWorldPosition(journeyPointA);
        objects[DEPTH_ORDER[i1]]!.getWorldPosition(journeyPointB);
        const standoffA = standoffFor(DEPTH_ORDER[i0]);
        const standoffB = standoffFor(DEPTH_ORDER[i1]);
        const gap = journeyPointA.distanceTo(journeyPointB);
        if (i0 === i1 || gap <= 0) {
          objectWorldPos.copy(journeyPointA);
          standoff = standoffA;
        } else {
          // Geometric on both terms, so each is a straight line in log space.
          standoff = Math.exp(THREE.MathUtils.lerp(Math.log(standoffA), Math.log(standoffB), frac));
          const startDistance = gap + standoffA;
          const distance = startDistance * Math.pow(standoffB / startDistance, frac);
          // The camera sits `standoff` in front of the look-at point, so
          // solve for where the look-at has to be for the camera to end up
          // `distance` from the destination.
          const along = THREE.MathUtils.clamp(1 - (distance - standoff) / gap, 0, 1);
          objectWorldPos.lerpVectors(journeyPointA, journeyPointB, along);
        }
      } else if (focusKey) {
        objects[focusKey]!.getWorldPosition(objectWorldPos);
        standoff = standoffFor(focusKey) * (cosmosMode === "backdrop" ? backdropStandoffMultiplier() : 1);
        if (cosmosMode === "backdrop") {
          const t = approachProgress() * approachScale();
          standoff *= THREE.MathUtils.lerp(1, APPROACH_CLOSE, t);
          framedYaw = APPROACH_YAW * t;
          framedPitch = APPROACH_PITCH * t;
        }
      } else if (isDesktop) {
        // Homepage with the loop never running (reduced motion): settle on
        // the first waypoint rather than the origin, which is now empty
        // space 400 units short of the nearest object.
        objects[DEPTH_ORDER[0]]!.getWorldPosition(objectWorldPos);
        standoff = standoffFor(DEPTH_ORDER[0]);
      } else {
        objectWorldPos.set(0, 0, 0);
        standoff = IDLE_CAMERA_Z;
      }
      // Mouse tilt pans the whole camera rather than only re-aiming it, and
      // by an amount proportional to the standoff. That's what turns it
      // into real parallax: the same world-space sway subtends a large
      // angle for the object being visited and almost nothing for one a
      // thousand units further out.
      //
      // The framing offset is applied along the camera's *right* vector,
      // not along world x. With no orbit the two are the same thing and
      // this reduces to the original expression; once the approach starts
      // swinging the camera round they are not, and offsetting in world x
      // would let the object drift back toward the centre of the frame -
      // i.e. onto the text - by cos(yaw) as the leg played out. Every
      // contrast guarantee on these pages rests on the object staying put
      // in frame, so the offset has to rotate with the camera.
      const offsetX = framingOffsetX(standoff);
      const rightX = Math.cos(framedYaw);
      const rightZ = -Math.sin(framedYaw);
      out.set(
        objectWorldPos.x - offsetX * rightX + (tiltY + dragTiltY) * standoff * 0.35,
        objectWorldPos.y - (tiltX + dragTiltX) * standoff * 0.35,
        objectWorldPos.z - offsetX * rightZ,
      );
      return standoff;
    }

    if (!reduceMotion) {
      window.addEventListener("mousemove", (e) => {
        // Suppressed mid-drag. The pointer is being used to say something
        // specific at that moment, and letting the ambient sway keep
        // tracking it as well makes the same motion count twice.
        if (dragging) return;
        const nx = e.clientX / window.innerWidth - 0.5;
        const ny = e.clientY / window.innerHeight - 0.5;
        targetTiltY = nx * 0.12;
        targetTiltX = ny * 0.08;
      });

      // ---- Drag to look around ----
      //
      // The scene had exactly one thing you could do to it - scroll - and
      // scrolling is the site's navigation, so the objects themselves were
      // never anything you could touch. Dragging swings the camera around
      // whatever it is framing and springs back when you let go, which is
      // enough to make them read as things in a space rather than as
      // pictures of things.
      //
      // It reuses the tilt channel above rather than adding a second
      // camera concept: tilt already pans the whole rig by an amount
      // proportional to the standoff, which is what makes it real parallax
      // instead of a swivel, and a drag is the same gesture with a wider
      // range and a memory.
      //
      // Homepage only. There, <main> is a scroll track with a menu in it
      // and the objects are the content. On an inner page the scene is a
      // backdrop behind prose, and a drag across prose means selecting
      // text - taking that over would be a straight loss.
      const DRAG_SENSITIVITY = 0.0011;
      const DRAG_LIMIT = 0.3;
      window.addEventListener("pointerdown", (e) => {
        if (cosmosMode !== "hero" || dragging) return;
        // Touch and pen are left alone: on a touch screen this same gesture
        // is the scroll that drives the entire journey.
        if (e.pointerType !== "mouse" || e.button !== 0) return;
        const target = e.target as HTMLElement | null;
        if (target?.closest("a, button, input, textarea, select, [role='button']")) return;
        dragging = true;
        dragPointer = e.pointerId;
        dragLastX = e.clientX;
        dragLastY = e.clientY;
        container!.dataset.cosmosDrag = "true";
      });

      const endDrag = (e: PointerEvent) => {
        if (!dragging || e.pointerId !== dragPointer) return;
        dragging = false;
        delete container!.dataset.cosmosDrag;
      };
      window.addEventListener("pointerup", endDrag);
      window.addEventListener("pointercancel", endDrag);

      window.addEventListener("pointermove", (e) => {
        if (!dragging || e.pointerId !== dragPointer) return;
        // Both signs negative, so the scene follows the pointer: the camera
        // moves opposite to what it frames, and a drag that pushed the
        // object the other way would feel like fighting it.
        dragTiltY = THREE.MathUtils.clamp(
          dragTiltY - (e.clientX - dragLastX) * DRAG_SENSITIVITY,
          -DRAG_LIMIT,
          DRAG_LIMIT,
        );
        dragTiltX = THREE.MathUtils.clamp(
          dragTiltX + (e.clientY - dragLastY) * DRAG_SENSITIVITY,
          -DRAG_LIMIT,
          DRAG_LIMIT,
        );
        dragLastX = e.clientX;
        dragLastY = e.clientY;
      });

      document.addEventListener("cosmos:focus", ((e: CustomEvent<{ key: CosmosKey }>) => {
        if (objects[e.detail.key]) focusKey = e.detail.key;
      }) as EventListener);
      document.addEventListener("cosmos:blur", () => {
        focusKey = null;
      });

      // Distinct from focus above: this rides on top of whatever the scroll
      // journey has already decided, rather than replacing it, so it works
      // on the homepage where focus does not.
      document.addEventListener("cosmos:hover", ((e: CustomEvent<{ key: CosmosKey | null }>) => {
        const key = e.detail.key;
        hoverKey = key !== null && objects[key] ? key : null;
      }) as EventListener);
    }

    // ---- Route-based arrival framing: which object (if any) a given page
    // is "about". The homepage has no entry (idle wide shot); every inner
    // page settles the camera on its section's object, same mechanism as
    // hover-focus above, just sticky instead of cleared on mouseleave
    // (inner pages have no HomeMenu to dispatch cosmos:blur). Reused rather
    // than a separate code path, since "arriving settled on an object" and
    // "hovering to preview an object" are visually the same shot. ----
    function normalizedPath(): string {
      const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL || "/";
      let path = location.pathname;
      if (base !== "/" && path.startsWith(base)) path = "/" + path.slice(base.length);
      return path;
    }

    function currentSectionKey(): CosmosKey | null {
      const seg = normalizedPath().replace(/^\/+|\/+$/g, "").split("/")[0] as CosmosKey;
      return objects[seg] ? seg : null;
    }

    // ---- Click-warp: navigation as a continuous move through one world,
    // not a cut between two pages. HomeMenu preventDefaults the click and
    // dispatches this instead, so we control exactly when navigation fires
    // relative to the camera rather than racing a real link click against
    // our own animation. Skipped entirely under reduced motion (HomeMenu
    // never dispatches it in that case; plain links just work).
    //
    // What happens, in order: the camera drops into a fast dive toward the
    // clicked object while the page's own content dissolves (see the "Page
    // transit" block in base.css); the route swaps while the camera is
    // right up against it; then applyRouteFraming clears warpKey and hands
    // back a normal resting standoff, so the rig eases back *out* of the
    // dive with the destination page materializing around it.
    //
    // The white flash this replaced existed to hide the swap. Nothing needs
    // hiding now - you never leave the scene, so there is no seam. ----
    const WARP_NAV_DELAY_MS = 460;
    // A tighter lerp than the idle 0.06: the dive has to substantially
    // complete inside WARP_NAV_DELAY_MS, or the swap lands while the camera
    // is still ambling and the arrival has no "burst out" to ease from.
    const WARP_EASE = 0.13;
    // Only the arrival half of the animation is conditional on having
    // departed: a cold load or a back-button arrival should just be there,
    // not fade itself in from nothing after the content has already painted.
    let departed = false;
    let warpWatchdog = 0;

    function setPageTransit(state: "depart" | "arrive" | null) {
      if (reduceMotion) return;
      if (state) container!.dataset.pageTransit = state;
      else delete container!.dataset.pageTransit;
    }

    document.addEventListener("cosmos:warp", ((e: CustomEvent<{ key: CosmosKey; href: string; reload?: boolean }>) => {
      // A dive is already in flight - a second click would queue a second
      // navigation behind the first and land somewhere the visitor didn't
      // ask for. Guarded on `departed` rather than `warpKey` so it also
      // covers the tiers that have no objects to dive toward.
      if (departed) return;
      // ...but never permanently. `departed` is normally cleared by
      // astro:page-load on the other side; if that navigation never
      // arrives - offline, a failed fetch, a blocked route - every
      // subsequent click would be swallowed and the menu would look dead
      // with no way back short of a reload. Release it either way.
      window.clearTimeout(warpWatchdog);
      warpWatchdog = window.setTimeout(() => {
        departed = false;
        warpKey = null;
        setPageTransit(null);
      }, WARP_NAV_DELAY_MS + 5000);
      const { key, href, reload } = e.detail;
      if (objects[key]) {
        focusKey = key;
        warpKey = key;
      }
      departed = true;
      setPageTransit("depart");
      window.setTimeout(() => {
        // Playground runs its own Three.js scenes and needs a real document
        // load; it still gets the dive, it just can't keep the WebGL context
        // on the other side of it.
        if (reload) window.location.href = href;
        else navigate(href);
      }, WARP_NAV_DELAY_MS);
    }) as EventListener);

    function applyRouteFraming(instant: boolean) {
      focusKey = currentSectionKey();
      const path = normalizedPath().replace(/\/+$/, "");
      cosmosMode = path === "" ? "hero" : "backdrop";
      container!.dataset.cosmosMode = cosmosMode;
      // <main> has just been swapped, so the column this route actually
      // renders - and therefore how much of the viewport the glare shield is
      // allowed to cover - is only knowable now.
      measureColumn();
      // Releasing the dive here rather than on a timer is what makes the
      // arrival read as one continuous move: the camera is deep inside the
      // object's space at this instant, and simply handing the rig a normal
      // resting standoff lets it ease back out under its own momentum.
      warpKey = null;
      window.clearTimeout(warpWatchdog);
      if (departed) {
        departed = false;
        // The incoming content is still at the departed opacity (the state
        // lives on this persisted element, so it survived the swap). Flip it
        // to the arrival pose, then release it a frame later so the browser
        // has a computed "from" value to transition out of - setting and
        // clearing in the same frame would just be a no-op.
        setPageTransit("arrive");
        requestAnimationFrame(() => requestAnimationFrame(() => setPageTransit(null)));
      } else {
        setPageTransit(null);
      }
      const standoff = computeCameraTarget(framedTarget);
      // Is the camera close enough to this object for easing toward it to
      // mean anything?
      //
      // The rig's lerp is exponential, so what it leaves behind is a
      // *proportional* error - and the two quantities here are wildly out
      // of scale with each other. A cold load starts the camera 8000 units
      // from Contact, which wants to be viewed from under four; easing that
      // in still leaves the camera tens of units short long after the page
      // has settled, and the probe renders as a speck in the middle of the
      // screen instead of framed in the margin. Nobody flew anywhere in
      // that case - the visitor typed a URL or clicked a header link - so
      // there is no journey to preserve and the honest thing is to be there
      // already.
      //
      // A warp arrival is the case this deliberately excludes: the camera
      // is right up against the object at that moment, well inside the
      // ratio, so it keeps easing out of the dive as Tier 2 intends.
      const ROUTE_SNAP_RATIO = 25;
      const arrivingFromFar = camera.position.distanceTo(framedTarget) > standoff * ROUTE_SNAP_RATIO;

      if (!instant && !arrivingFromFar) return;

      // Reduced motion never runs the animate() loop below, so the eased
      // approach never happens on its own — snap directly to the settled
      // shot and render exactly one frame, same policy as the idle scene.
      if (instant) {
        ALL_KEYS.forEach((key) => {
          dimBase[key] = focusKey === null ? 1 : key === focusKey ? 1 : DIM_FLOOR;
          blurBase[key] = focusKey === null ? 0 : key === focusKey ? 0 : 1;
          hoverCurrent[key] = 0;
          dimCurrent[key] = dimBase[key];
          blurCurrent[key] = blurBase[key];
        });
      }
      cameraRig.setTarget(framedTarget, standoff, framedYaw, framedPitch);
      cameraRig.snapToTarget();
      starfield.group.position.copy(camera.position);
      if (instant) {
        // A whole second of easing in one call, which settles the shield's
        // fade completely. Reduced motion never runs animate(), so this is
        // the only chance it gets - without it those visitors keep the text
        // over the sun and none of the shade.
        updateGlare(1);
        renderFrame();
      }
    }
    // Fires on the first load and on every subsequent client-side
    // navigation alike once <ClientRouter /> is active in BaseLayout; a
    // no-op before that lands, since nothing dispatches it yet.
    document.addEventListener("astro:page-load", () => applyRouteFraming(reduceMotion));

    // Screen footprint of a waypoint, in drawing-buffer pixels with the
    // origin at the bottom left, padded for the blur that is about to be
    // spread over it.
    //
    // Every pass below is scissored to this. A defocused waypoint is a
    // small bright patch on a large dark frame, and without this each one
    // was paying for a full-screen clear, a full-screen composite and five
    // full-screen blur taps to move a few hundred pixels around. The pad
    // is generous on purpose: the blur samples outside the rect, and what
    // is outside is whatever the previous layer left there.
    const scissorCenter = new THREE.Vector3();
    const scissorRect = new THREE.Vector4();
    function computeScissor(key: CosmosKey, blurPx: number, width: number, height: number) {
      const extent = objectExtent[key];
      objects[key]!.getWorldPosition(scissorCenter);
      scissorCenter.applyMatrix4(camera.matrixWorldInverse);
      const distance = -scissorCenter.z;
      const radius = extent ? Math.hypot(extent.x, extent.y, extent.z) / 2 : 0;
      // Behind the camera, or close enough to be around it: no meaningful
      // rectangle exists, so take the whole frame and pay for it. Rare, and
      // wrong in the safe direction.
      if (distance <= radius * 1.2 || distance <= camera.near) {
        scissorRect.set(0, 0, width, height);
        return scissorRect;
      }
      // Perspective divide by hand rather than via project(), which would
      // need the centre again in world space.
      const halfHeight = Math.tan((camera.fov * Math.PI) / 360) * distance;
      const halfWidth = halfHeight * camera.aspect;
      const cx = (scissorCenter.x / halfWidth * 0.5 + 0.5) * width;
      const cy = (scissorCenter.y / halfHeight * 0.5 + 0.5) * height;
      const pad = (radius / halfHeight) * (height / 2) + blurPx * 3 + 8;
      const x0 = Math.max(0, Math.floor(cx - pad));
      const y0 = Math.max(0, Math.floor(cy - pad));
      const x1 = Math.min(width, Math.ceil(cx + pad));
      const y1 = Math.min(height, Math.ceil(cy + pad));
      scissorRect.set(x0, y0, Math.max(0, x1 - x0), Math.max(0, y1 - y0));
      return scissorRect;
    }

    // Two different scissors, because the renderer keeps two.
    //
    // An offscreen target carries its own, read once by setRenderTarget -
    // so it has to be set on the target, before switching to it.
    // renderer.setScissor() is only consulted when drawing to the canvas,
    // and it takes CSS pixels: it multiplies by the pixel ratio itself. The
    // rect here is in drawing-buffer pixels, so that has to be divided back
    // out. Passing device pixels to it puts the rectangle at twice the
    // intended offset and size, which on a retina display means somewhere
    // off the bottom right of the screen - and the layer composites into
    // nothing at all.
    function scissorTo(rect: THREE.Vector4, target: THREE.WebGLRenderTarget | null, scale: number) {
      const x = Math.floor(rect.x * scale);
      const y = Math.floor(rect.y * scale);
      const w = Math.ceil(rect.z * scale);
      const h = Math.ceil(rect.w * scale);
      if (target) {
        target.scissor.set(x, y, w, h);
        target.scissorTest = true;
        renderer.setRenderTarget(target);
        return;
      }
      const ratio = renderer.getPixelRatio();
      renderer.setScissor(x / ratio, y / ratio, w / ratio, h / ratio);
      renderer.setScissorTest(true);
      renderer.setRenderTarget(null);
    }

    // Wipes all three targets outside the scissor as well as inside, once
    // per layer. The scissor bounds what each pass *draws*; it does not
    // bound what the blur *samples*, and the taps reach far enough past the
    // rectangle to pick up whatever the previous layer left there. Since
    // the composite adds, that arrives as a ghost of the last object,
    // fringing the edge of this one. Clears are bandwidth, not shading -
    // cheap enough that bounding them is not worth the correctness risk.
    function clearLayerTargets() {
      for (const target of [rtLayer!, rtPing!, rtPong!]) {
        target.scissorTest = false;
        renderer.setRenderTarget(target);
        renderer.clear(true, true, true);
      }
    }

    function blurPass(
      source: THREE.Texture,
      target: THREE.WebGLRenderTarget,
      spacing: number,
      horizontal: boolean,
      rect: THREE.Vector4,
    ) {
      quad.material = blurMaterial;
      blurMaterial.uniforms.tDiffuse.value = source;
      // Offsets are in target texels either way: for the separable pair the
      // two targets are the same size, and for the first read out of the
      // full-resolution target sampling at the coarser pitch is the point.
      blurMaterial.uniforms.uStep.value.set(
        horizontal ? spacing / target.width : 0,
        horizontal ? 0 : spacing / target.height,
      );
      scissorTo(rect, target, DEFOCUS_BLUR_SCALE);
      renderer.render(quadScene, quadCamera);
    }

    function renderFrame() {
      // Nothing defocused - no journey, reduced motion, a mobile tier with
      // no waypoints at all - so the whole scene is one ordinary pass and
      // none of the machinery below is paid for.
      let layered = false;
      for (const key of ALL_KEYS) {
        if (objects[key]!.visible && blurCurrent[key] > DEFOCUS_MIN) {
          layered = true;
          break;
        }
      }
      if (!layered) {
        renderer.render(scene, camera);
        return;
      }

      sizeDefocusTargets();
      defocusLayers.length = 0;
      ALL_KEYS.forEach((key, i) => {
        const group = objects[key]!;
        defocusWasVisible[i] = group.visible;
        if (!group.visible || blurCurrent[key] <= DEFOCUS_MIN) return;
        group.getWorldPosition(cullWorldPos);
        defocusLayers.push({ key, distance: cullWorldPos.distanceTo(camera.position) });
      });
      defocusLayers.sort((a, b) => b.distance - a.distance);
      // A hard ceiling on what one frame will spend softening things. Two
      // is what the journey actually produces - the waypoint behind and the
      // one ahead - and anything past that is far enough to be a few dim
      // pixels, which the haze has already taken most of the way to
      // nothing. Drop the furthest, since those are the ones that cost the
      // most relative to what they contribute.
      if (defocusLayers.length > DEFOCUS_MAX_LAYERS) {
        defocusLayers.splice(0, defocusLayers.length - DEFOCUS_MAX_LAYERS);
      }

      renderer.autoClear = false;

      // Pass one: the room. Stars and transit dust, never defocused - they
      // are not somewhere you are travelling to.
      anchor.visible = false;
      renderer.setRenderTarget(null);
      renderer.clear();
      renderer.render(scene, camera);
      anchor.visible = true;

      // Pass two: one softened object at a time, back to front.
      starfield.group.visible = false;
      skyEvents.object.visible = false;
      const dust = transitDust ? transitDust.object : null;
      if (dust) dust.visible = false;

      for (const layer of defocusLayers) {
        // Sigma is asked for in CSS pixels, so it converts through the
        // device pixel ratio and then through the blur target's own scale.
        const blurPx = DEFOCUS_MAX_PX * renderer.getPixelRatio() * blurCurrent[layer.key];
        const rect = computeScissor(layer.key, blurPx, drawSize.x, drawSize.y);
        if (rect.z <= 0 || rect.w <= 0) continue;
        // Two separable passes at spacings s and 0.6s compose to a sigma of
        // about 2.33s; solve back for the spacing that lands on the sigma
        // asked for.
        const spacing = Math.max(0.35, (blurPx * DEFOCUS_BLUR_SCALE) / 2.33);

        ALL_KEYS.forEach((key) => {
          objects[key]!.visible = key === layer.key;
        });
        clearLayerTargets();
        scissorTo(rect, rtLayer, DEFOCUS_LAYER_SCALE);
        renderer.render(scene, camera);

        // The first horizontal pass reads the half-resolution layer and
        // writes the quarter-resolution one, so it is the downsample as
        // well. Its taps reach about two source texels either side, which
        // is wide enough that a 2:1 reduction doesn't alias - the reason
        // this used to be a separate box filter.
        blurPass(rtLayer!.texture, rtPing!, spacing * 0.6, true, rect);
        blurPass(rtPing!.texture, rtPong!, spacing * 0.6, false, rect);
        blurPass(rtPong!.texture, rtPing!, spacing, true, rect);
        blurPass(rtPing!.texture, rtPong!, spacing, false, rect);

        quad.material = compositeMaterial;
        // rtPong, because the last of the four passes wrote it. Folding the
        // downsample into the first pass moved the ping-pong by one, and
        // reading rtPing here was reading the state before the final
        // vertical blur - a horizontally-smeared layer.
        compositeMaterial.uniforms.tBlur.value = rtPong!.texture;
        scissorTo(rect, null, 1);
        renderer.render(quadScene, quadCamera);
      }
      renderer.setScissorTest(false);

      // Pass three: everything still sharp, straight onto the frame at full
      // resolution with the canvas's own multisampling - the ordinary path,
      // drawing the ordinary way. Over the softened layers rather than
      // under them, which is the right order because every softened object
      // is further from the camera than the one in focus.
      ALL_KEYS.forEach((key, i) => {
        objects[key]!.visible = defocusWasVisible[i] && blurCurrent[key] <= DEFOCUS_MIN;
      });
      renderer.render(scene, camera);

      ALL_KEYS.forEach((key, i) => {
        objects[key]!.visible = defocusWasVisible[i];
      });
      starfield.group.visible = true;
      skyEvents.object.visible = true;
      if (dust) dust.visible = true;
      renderer.setRenderTarget(null);
      renderer.autoClear = true;
    }

    // ---- The star's mass ejections ----
    const FLARE_GAP: [number, number] = [45, 95];
    const FLARE_LIFE = 5.5;
    const FLARE_UP = new THREE.Vector3(0, 1, 0);
    const flareDir = new THREE.Vector3();
    const flareCenter = new THREE.Vector3();
    const flareView = new THREE.Vector3();
    let flareWait = 12 + Math.random() * 20;
    let flareT = -1;

    // How far the star's mass ejection currently reaches, weighted by how
    // bright it is: 0 when there is no eruption. Read by updateGlare.
    let flareGlare = 0;

    function updateFlare(dt: number, dim: number) {
      const about = objects.about;
      if (!about) return;
      const flare = about.userData.flare as THREE.Group;
      const puffs = about.userData.flarePuffs as THREE.Sprite[];

      if (flareT < 0) {
        flareWait -= dt;
        if (flareWait > 0) return;
        flareT = 0;
        // A fresh direction each time - real ejections come off active
        // regions, and since the star rotates here, that is closer to the
        // truth than any fixed set of launch sites.
        //
        // Then flattened toward the plane of the sky. A direction sampled
        // freely on the sphere points at or away from the camera as often
        // as not, and a plume projected onto a disc that already fills a
        // third of the frame is a smudge on a bright surface - which is
        // what the first version looked like. Seen from the side it clears
        // the limb against dark sky, which is also how every photograph of
        // one is taken.
        flareDir.set(Math.random() * 2 - 1, Math.random() * 2 - 1, Math.random() * 2 - 1).normalize();
        about.getWorldPosition(flareCenter);
        flareView.copy(camera.position).sub(flareCenter).normalize();
        flareDir.addScaledVector(flareView, -flareDir.dot(flareView) * 0.85).normalize();
        flare.quaternion.setFromUnitVectors(FLARE_UP, flareDir);
        flare.visible = true;
      }

      flareT += dt;
      if (flareT >= FLARE_LIFE) {
        flareT = -1;
        flareWait = FLARE_GAP[0] + Math.random() * (FLARE_GAP[1] - FLARE_GAP[0]);
        flare.visible = false;
        flareGlare = 0;
        return;
      }

      const p = flareT / FLARE_LIFE;
      // Leaves the limb fast and decelerates, rather than travelling at a
      // constant rate - which is both what the material does against the
      // star's gravity and what keeps the launch readable.
      const reach = 0.5 + Math.pow(p, 0.55) * 1.9;
      flare.position.copy(flareDir).multiplyScalar(reach);
      flare.scale.setScalar(1 + p * 1.6);
      // Fades in over the first tenth and spends the rest dying, so it
      // erupts rather than materializing.
      const envelope = Math.min(1, p * 10) * Math.pow(1 - p, 1.5);
      puffs.forEach((puff, i) => {
        const trail = 1 - (i / puffs.length) * 0.55;
        (puff.material as THREE.SpriteMaterial).opacity = envelope * trail * dim;
      });
      // Published for the glare shield. The shield sizes itself from a
      // bounding extent measured once at construction, and the ejection is
      // not in it - it erupts a couple of star-radii past the limb, right
      // out into the column the text is written in. Measured over 45s, the
      // About list sat at 7.6:1 and dropped to 3.9:1 for the length of an
      // eruption: 5.5 seconds, every 45 to 95, which is long enough to be
      // reading through and often enough to meet.
      flareGlare = envelope * (reach / 2.4);
    }

    // ---- The pulsar's flash, aimed ----
    //
    // The beat used to be Math.sin(clock * 2.4), which had nothing to do
    // with where the beams were pointing: the object flashed on its own
    // schedule while two cones swept past on theirs, and the one thing that
    // makes a pulsar a pulsar - that you see it *because* a beam crossed
    // you - was missing. This derives the flash from the real geometry
    // instead. It is the same event now.
    const BEAM_RATE = 1.2; // rad/s about the object's axis
    const beamAxis = new THREE.Vector3();
    const toCamera = new THREE.Vector3();
    const beamCenter = new THREE.Vector3();
    let beamPhase = 0;
    let aimWindowMax = 0;
    let aimWindowMin = 1;
    let aimMax = 1;
    let aimMin = 0;

    function beamFlash(dt: number): number {
      const group = objects.music;
      if (!group) return 0;
      const beamGroup = group.userData.beamGroup as THREE.Object3D;
      beamGroup.updateWorldMatrix(true, false);
      // The cones are built along the group's local x, so this is the beam
      // axis; the two of them are opposite, which is why the sign is
      // dropped rather than clamped.
      beamAxis.set(1, 0, 0).transformDirection(beamGroup.matrixWorld);
      group.getWorldPosition(beamCenter);
      toCamera.copy(camera.position).sub(beamCenter).normalize();
      const aim = Math.abs(beamAxis.dot(toCamera));

      // How close the beam comes to the viewer depends entirely on where
      // the viewer is: from most angles the sweep never points anywhere
      // near you, and a raw dot product would leave the pulsar permanently
      // dark. So the flash is normalized against the range this sweep
      // actually covers, sampled over the previous half-turn - two beams,
      // so half a turn is one full cycle of the geometry.
      aimWindowMax = Math.max(aimWindowMax, aim);
      aimWindowMin = Math.min(aimWindowMin, aim);
      beamPhase += BEAM_RATE * dt;
      if (beamPhase >= Math.PI) {
        beamPhase -= Math.PI;
        aimMax = aimWindowMax;
        aimMin = aimWindowMin;
        aimWindowMax = 0;
        aimWindowMin = 1;
      }

      const span = aimMax - aimMin;
      // A viewer sitting on the rotation axis sees no variation at all, and
      // dividing by that span would turn sampling noise into a strobe.
      // Hold a steady half-lit state instead, which is also the honest
      // answer: nothing is sweeping past you.
      const t = span > 0.02 ? THREE.MathUtils.clamp((aim - aimMin) / span, 0, 1) : 0.5;
      // Steep, so the beam has to be close to pointed at you before
      // anything happens - a sharp beat rather than a breathing glow.
      return Math.pow(t, 12);
    }

    function animate(now: number) {
      // Real elapsed time, clamped (see timing.ts). Everything below that
      // used to be a per-frame constant is expressed against this, so the
      // scene runs at the same speed on a 60Hz panel, a 120Hz ProMotion
      // display and a machine struggling at 30.
      const dt = prevFrameTime === 0 ? 1 / 60 : Math.min(MAX_STEP, (now - prevFrameTime) / 1000);
      prevFrameTime = now;
      clock += dt;
      const inJourney = journeyActive();
      const journeyProgress = inJourney ? journeyDepthProgress() : 0;
      // The pair of waypoints the current leg runs between - the only two
      // objects that are ever deliberately being looked at mid-journey.
      const journeyFrom = Math.floor(journeyProgress);
      const journeyTo = Math.min(journeyFrom + 1, DEPTH_ORDER.length - 1);
      const tiltEase = easeFactor(0.05, dt);
      tiltX += (targetTiltX - tiltX) * tiltEase;
      tiltY += (targetTiltY - tiltY) * tiltEase;
      // Spring-back. Slower than the ambient tilt, so releasing a drag
      // reads as the camera settling rather than snapping home - and it is
      // only applied once the drag is over, so what is held stays held.
      if (!dragging) {
        const release = easeFactor(0.035, dt);
        dragTiltX += (0 - dragTiltX) * release;
        dragTiltY += (0 - dragTiltY) * release;
      }
      // The anchor is no longer rotated. It used to carry a slow idle drift
      // plus the mouse tilt, which was harmless when every object sat within
      // 8 units of it - at 8000 units out, a tenth of a radian would sling
      // the far waypoints hundreds of units across the sky. Tilt now moves
      // the camera instead (see computeCameraTarget).

      const dimEase = easeFactor(0.08, dt);
      // Shorter than the dim/blur lag on purpose. A hover is a direct answer
      // to something the visitor just did; at 0.08 it arrived late enough to
      // read as the scene noticing rather than as the scene responding.
      const hoverEase = easeFactor(0.16, dt);
      ALL_KEYS.forEach((key) => {
        let target: number;
        let blurTarget: number;
        if (inJourney) {
          const dist = Math.abs(journeyProgress - DEPTH_ORDER.indexOf(key));
          target = THREE.MathUtils.clamp(1 - dist * DIM_FALLOFF, DIM_FLOOR, 1);
          blurTarget = THREE.MathUtils.smoothstep(dist, DEFOCUS_START, DEFOCUS_FULL);
        } else {
          target = focusKey === null ? 1 : key === focusKey ? 1 : DIM_FLOOR;
          blurTarget = focusKey === null ? 0 : key === focusKey ? 0 : 1;
        }
        const hovered = objects[key]!;
        hoverCurrent[key] += ((hoverKey === key ? 1 : 0) - hoverCurrent[key]) * hoverEase;
        dimBase[key] += (target - dimBase[key]) * dimEase;
        blurBase[key] += (blurTarget - blurBase[key]) * dimEase;
        // The hover rides on the eased values rather than being folded into
        // them: an exponential lerp accumulates on its own previous output,
        // so multiplying the state itself would feed the lift back into the
        // next frame's starting point and compound it.
        dimCurrent[key] = dimBase[key] * (1 + HOVER_GAIN * hoverCurrent[key]);
        // Sharpening as well as brightening, so a hovered waypoint reads
        // as the thing you are about to travel to rather than as the same
        // smudge with the contrast turned up.
        blurCurrent[key] = blurBase[key] * (1 - hoverCurrent[key]);
        // And a lean toward the viewer, which is the part that actually
        // registers. Brightness alone was close to invisible here: the
        // label you can point at is by construction the waypoint you are
        // already parked on, so it is already at full brightness and zero
        // blur, and every opacity the lift feeds clamps at 1. Measured
        // total frame luminance did not move. Scale has no such ceiling.
        //
        // Safe against the framing maths: group scale is set once at
        // construction and placeObjects() only ever touches position, and
        // six percent is well inside the padding the defocus scissor
        // already carries.
        if (hoverCurrent[key] > 0.001 || hovered.scale.x !== OBJECT_LAYOUT[key].scale) {
          hovered.scale.setScalar(OBJECT_LAYOUT[key].scale * (1 + HOVER_LEAN * hoverCurrent[key]));
        }
        // Note: no scale normalization here any more. Apparent size is now
        // purely a function of true size and distance, which is the entire
        // point - it's what lets a galaxy read as a galaxy and a probe as a
        // probe rather than as six equally-sized ornaments.

        // Drop objects that have shrunk past the point of contributing
        // anything. This matters far more here than in a conventional
        // scene: the objects span 8000 units of depth, so at any moment
        // four or five of the six are hundreds or thousands of units away
        // and measure a fraction of a pixel - while still paying for every
        // vertex and every fragment of a 20k-triangle spacecraft or a 9200
        // point galaxy. Keyed off real projected size rather than a fixed
        // distance, since "far" means something different for each of them.
        const group = objects[key]!;
        // Never cull what you are travelling to or from, however small it
        // currently is. The threshold below is about not paying for specks,
        // but the destination of a leg *starts* as a speck - hiding it
        // outright is what turned the long approach to Contact into an
        // empty screen instead of a distant object growing closer.
        //
        // Only while actually travelling, though. journeyProgress sits on a
        // whole number for the length of each waypoint's dwell, and
        // exempting the *next* waypoint through that hold put a spiral
        // galaxy in the middle of the solar system - far enough to be a
        // smudge, near enough to read as part of the thing you'd stopped to
        // look at. Parked, the size threshold decides, which is what it's
        // for.
        const travelling = inJourney && journeyProgress - journeyFrom > 0.001;
        const isActive =
          key === warpKey ||
          key === focusKey ||
          (inJourney && key === DEPTH_ORDER[journeyFrom]) ||
          (travelling && key === DEPTH_ORDER[journeyTo]);
        group.getWorldPosition(cullWorldPos);
        const distance = cullWorldPos.distanceTo(camera.position);
        const extent = objectExtent[key];
        group.visible =
          isActive || !extent || distance <= 0 || extent.y / distance > CULL_ANGULAR_SIZE;
      });

      const standoff = computeCameraTarget(framedTarget);
      cameraRig.setTarget(framedTarget, standoff, framedYaw, framedPitch);
      // The idle 0.06 is a ~270ms lag to catch 63% of a move. That is fine
      // for a hover preview and far too slow for a scroll-driven camera:
      // scrolling at any speed left the rig permanently chasing a target it
      // never reached, so no object was ever actually framed. The journey
      // gets a tighter lerp; the dwell curve above is what keeps it from
      // feeling snappy, since during a hold the target isn't moving at all.
      cameraRig.update(warpKey ? WARP_EASE : inJourney ? JOURNEY_EASE : undefined, dt);

      // Stars are the one thing that should never fall behind: the camera
      // travels ~7800 units, far outside the fixed shells they're built on,
      // so the whole field rides along and surrounds the viewer throughout.
      updateGlare(dt);

      starfield.group.position.copy(camera.position);
      // The approach swings the star field as well as the camera, and this
      // is the one piece of it that is *not* gated to the desktop scene.
      //
      // Below 900px there are no six objects to close on - that gate is in
      // deviceTier - so without this an inner page on a phone would have
      // the approach scale telling the visitor they are travelling and
      // nothing behind the words agreeing. The layers rotate by their own
      // parallaxFactor, so the near field swings further than the far one
      // and the result reads as depth rather than as a rotating backdrop.
      // On desktop it rides on top of the camera move and costs nothing.
      const swing = approachProgress() * APPROACH_SWING;
      starfield.update(dt, tiltX, tiltY + swing);
      skyEvents.update(dt, standoff, camera.position, cosmosMode === "backdrop");
      motionClock.advance(dt);
      if (transitDust) {
        transitDust.update(camera.position.z - prevCameraZ, standoff, camera.position, dt);
      }
      prevCameraZ = camera.position.z;

      if (isDesktop) {
      const about = objects.about!.userData;
      const starPulse = 1 + Math.sin(clock * 0.7) * 0.05;
      about.star.scale.setScalar(starPulse);
      about.coronaInner.material.opacity = (0.5 + Math.sin(clock * 0.9) * 0.1) * dimCurrent.about;
      about.coronaOuter.material.opacity = (0.24 + Math.sin(clock * 0.6 + 1) * 0.06) * dimCurrent.about;
      about.starMat.color.setScalar(0.45 + 0.55 * dimCurrent.about);
      updateFlare(dt, dimCurrent.about);

      // Eleven bodies on real orbital elements, so this one owns its own
      // step rather than having its internals poked from out here. It was
      // already integrating whatever step it was handed in seconds, so the
      // real delta is all it needed.
      objects.projects!.userData.update(dt, dimCurrent.projects);

      const music = objects.music!.userData;
      // 0.02/frame at 60Hz.
      music.beamGroup.rotation.y += BEAM_RATE * dt;
      const flash = beamFlash(dt);
      music.coreGlow.material.opacity = (0.5 + flash * 0.5) * dimCurrent.music;
      music.haloGlow.material.opacity = (0.22 + flash * 0.25) * dimCurrent.music;
      music.coreMat.emissiveIntensity = (1.2 + flash * 1.8) * dimCurrent.music;
      // The beams brighten on the beat too: the flash and the sweep are the
      // same event, which is the whole reason this object is Music.
      music.beamMat.opacity = (0.34 + flash * 0.34) * dimCurrent.music;
      music.discMat.opacity = (0.16 + flash * 0.14) * dimCurrent.music;

      const contact = objects.contact!.userData;
      // Slow and mostly about one axis: a real probe is attitude-stabilised
      // with its dish held on Earth, so a lazy drift reads right where a
      // free tumble would look like debris.
      contact.body.rotation.y += 0.21 * dt; // 0.0035/frame at 60Hz
      contact.body.rotation.x = Math.sin(clock * 0.25) * 0.12;
      contact.pings.forEach((ring: THREE.Mesh, i: number) => {
        const phase = ((clock + (i * contact.pingPeriod) / contact.pings.length) % contact.pingPeriod) / contact.pingPeriod;
        const scale = 0.15 + phase * 1.6;
        ring.scale.setScalar(scale);
        // Fade in as well as out, so a ring appears out of the spacecraft
        // rather than popping into existence at full strength on top of it.
        const envelope = Math.min(1, phase * 5) * (1 - phase);
        (ring.material as THREE.MeshBasicMaterial).opacity = envelope * 0.3 * dimCurrent.contact;
      });

      const playground = objects.playground!.userData;
      // No rotation. A nebula is light-years of gas; anything you could see
      // turning is wrong, and the old 0.0009/frame drift (a full revolution
      // every two minutes) was the single biggest reason the cloud read as
      // a spinning ornament. Its life now comes from parallax as the camera
      // moves through it, which is real motion rather than simulated.
      playground.points.material.opacity = 0.9 * dimCurrent.playground;

      const photography = objects.photography!.userData;
      // No rotation here any more. This used to turn the star cloud about
      // its z - which, since the disc is built in the xz plane, tipped the
      // galaxy end over end rather than spinning it. It now shears about
      // the disc's real axis, in the vertex shader, at a rate that falls
      // off with radius (see pointMotion.ts): the inner disc laps the
      // outer one and the arms trail, which is what a galaxy does and what
      // rigid rotation cannot look like at any speed.
      photography.points.material.opacity = 0.85 * dimCurrent.photography;
      photography.haze.material.opacity = 0.09 * dimCurrent.photography;
      photography.coreGlow.material.opacity = 0.42 * dimCurrent.photography;
      } // end if (isDesktop)

      renderFrame();
      requestAnimationFrame(animate);
    }

    if (reduceMotion) {
      renderFrame();
    } else {
      requestAnimationFrame(animate);
    }
  } catch (err) {
    // Leaving the pane empty is fine; leaving the *homepage* as it stands
    // is not. The journey is a tall scroll track whose only purpose is to
    // drive this camera, so without a scene it degrades to eight screens of
    // nothing with one link in it. Flagging the container lets HomeMenu's
    // CSS fall back to the plain section list, the same one mobile and
    // reduced-motion visitors already get.
    console.error(LOG, "Cosmos scene failed to initialize, falling back to the plain menu.", err);
    document.getElementById("home-cosmos")?.setAttribute("data-cosmos-failed", "true");
  }
</script>
